-- 1. Stop overselling across every storefront
INSERT INTO case_studies
  (title, slug, client, industry, engagement, summary, problem_lede, body_md, body_html,
   cover_image, cover_thumb, gallery, metrics, tech_tags, stack, meta_title, meta_description,
   sort_order, is_featured, is_published, published_at)
VALUES ('Stop overselling across every storefront', 'realtime-inventory-sync', 'Multi-store online retailer shipping from one warehouse', 'Retail & E-commerce', '6 weeks to first sync · ongoing support',
  'Several storefronts sold from one warehouse and oversold it daily. We compute one true sellable number from the ERP and push it to every store automatically.', 'Two customers bought the last unit within an hour of each other, on two different stores, because neither store knew the other had already sold it. Somebody had to phone one of them and apologise.', '### The problem

One warehouse. Several Shopify storefronts. One person exporting stock out of the ERP and pushing it into each store, when they got to it.

Everything went wrong in the gap. A unit sold on store A was still for sale on store B until the next export, so the same last piece got sold twice and somebody had to make the apology call. Purchase orders the supplier had not actually confirmed were being counted as stock, so the site promised goods that might never arrive. Units locked into in-progress quotations on the sales floor were invisible to the website. Products marked discontinued in the ERP stayed purchasable online. And when something sold out, someone had to remember to hide it — and remember again to unhide it when stock came back.

The team''s answer to all of this was a safety buffer: list less than you have. That cushion was inventory that could never be sold, held permanently, to compensate for a number nobody trusted.

### What we built

A service that computes the true sellable quantity for every product and pushes it to every store on a schedule, as often as every five minutes.

"True sellable" is one expression, and every term in it is defended:

```
sellable = max(0, on_hand + confirmed_po - in_progress - committed)
```

On-hand comes from the ERP. Only **supplier-confirmed** purchase orders count toward stock — unconfirmed quantities are still recorded, so a stock drop is always explainable, but they are never sold against. In-progress quotations are subtracted. And `committed` — units reserved by unfulfilled orders — is summed across **every** storefront, not per store, so a reservation anywhere is a reservation everywhere. That single decision is what ended the double-sell.

Two details earned their keep. Shopify keeps line items marked committed forever on orders closed without being fulfilled, so those phantom reservations are queried separately and subtracted back out — otherwise stock silently disappears and never returns. And every inventory write carries an idempotency key, so a retried batch can never double-apply a quantity. Rate limits are budgeted rather than discovered: the sync watches its remaining API credit and slows down before the platform throttles it.

Products that reach zero are unpublished automatically. Products that come back are republished automatically. Nobody has to remember.

### The result

No more hand-built stock files. The number on the website is the number in the warehouse, refreshed on a cycle the client controls — the minimum is five minutes, and most days they run it far more often than the old manual process ever managed.

The oversell calls stopped. Once they stopped, the safety buffer stopped being necessary, which is where the money actually was: inventory that had been held back permanently became sellable again.

Every product, every run, writes a full audit row — fifteen fields covering on-hand, confirmed and unconfirmed purchase orders, in-progress, committed, the old quantity, the new quantity and the action taken. When a merchandiser asks why an item went out of stock at three in the afternoon, the answer takes ten seconds instead of a meeting.

### Why it holds up

- **Idempotent writes and honest retries.** Five attempts with exponential backoff and jitter, throttling respected, and a unique idempotency key on every inventory write — a retry can never corrupt a count.
- **Degrades instead of failing.** A batch that fails falls back to per-item updates, so one bad product cannot take down two hundred and forty-nine good ones. A run that errors still writes everything it learned before it stopped.
- **Built for millions of rows.** Log purging runs in ten-thousand-row batches with pauses, indexes are built concurrently off the startup path, and row counts use table statistics rather than scanning.
- **One command to install, update or remove.** Docker Compose with healthchecks, database migrations that run at boot and are safe to re-run, and a stuck-sync watchdog that clears any run older than fifteen minutes.', '<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>One warehouse. Several Shopify storefronts. One person exporting stock out of the ERP and pushing it into each store, when they got to it.</p>
<p>Everything went wrong in the gap. A unit sold on store A was still for sale on store B until the next export, so the same last piece got sold twice and somebody had to make the apology call. Purchase orders the supplier had not actually confirmed were being counted as stock, so the site promised goods that might never arrive. Units locked into in-progress quotations on the sales floor were invisible to the website. Products marked discontinued in the ERP stayed purchasable online. And when something sold out, someone had to remember to hide it — and remember again to unhide it when stock came back.</p>
<p>The team&rsquo;s answer to all of this was a safety buffer: list less than you have. That cushion was inventory that could never be sold, held permanently, to compensate for a number nobody trusted.</p>
<h3>What we built</h3>
<p>A service that computes the true sellable quantity for every product and pushes it to every store on a schedule, as often as every five minutes.</p>
<p>&ldquo;True sellable&rdquo; is one expression, and every term in it is defended:</p>
<pre><code>sellable = max(0, on_hand + confirmed_po - in_progress - committed)
</code></pre>
<p>On-hand comes from the ERP. Only <strong>supplier-confirmed</strong> purchase orders count toward stock — unconfirmed quantities are still recorded, so a stock drop is always explainable, but they are never sold against. In-progress quotations are subtracted. And <code>committed</code> — units reserved by unfulfilled orders — is summed across <strong>every</strong> storefront, not per store, so a reservation anywhere is a reservation everywhere. That single decision is what ended the double-sell.</p>
<p>Two details earned their keep. Shopify keeps line items marked committed forever on orders closed without being fulfilled, so those phantom reservations are queried separately and subtracted back out — otherwise stock silently disappears and never returns. And every inventory write carries an idempotency key, so a retried batch can never double-apply a quantity. Rate limits are budgeted rather than discovered: the sync watches its remaining API credit and slows down before the platform throttles it.</p>
<p>Products that reach zero are unpublished automatically. Products that come back are republished automatically. Nobody has to remember.</p>
<h3>The result</h3>
<p>No more hand-built stock files. The number on the website is the number in the warehouse, refreshed on a cycle the client controls — the minimum is five minutes, and most days they run it far more often than the old manual process ever managed.</p>
<p>The oversell calls stopped. Once they stopped, the safety buffer stopped being necessary, which is where the money actually was: inventory that had been held back permanently became sellable again.</p>
<p>Every product, every run, writes a full audit row — fifteen fields covering on-hand, confirmed and unconfirmed purchase orders, in-progress, committed, the old quantity, the new quantity and the action taken. When a merchandiser asks why an item went out of stock at three in the afternoon, the answer takes ten seconds instead of a meeting.</p>
<h3>Why it holds up</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Idempotent writes and honest retries.</strong> Five attempts with exponential backoff and jitter, throttling respected, and a unique idempotency key on every inventory write — a retry can never corrupt a count.</li>
<li><strong>Degrades instead of failing.</strong> A batch that fails falls back to per-item updates, so one bad product cannot take down two hundred and forty-nine good ones. A run that errors still writes everything it learned before it stopped.</li>
<li><strong>Built for millions of rows.</strong> Log purging runs in ten-thousand-row batches with pauses, indexes are built concurrently off the startup path, and row counts use table statistics rather than scanning.</li>
<li><strong>One command to install, update or remove.</strong> Docker Compose with healthchecks, database migrations that run at boot and are safe to re-run, and a stuck-sync watchdog that clears any run older than fifteen minutes.</li>
</ul>',
