-- 2. From scan to printed label, one screen
INSERT INTO case_studies
  (title, slug, client, industry, engagement, summary, problem_lede, body_md, body_html,
   cover_image, cover_thumb, gallery, metrics, tech_tags, stack, meta_title, meta_description,
   sort_order, is_featured, is_published, published_at)
VALUES ('From scan to printed label, one screen', 'shipping-desk-consolidation', 'Wholesale distributor shipping from its own warehouse', 'Distribution & Wholesale', '8 weeks · ongoing support',
  'Packers retyped every address into a carrier portal, then retyped tracking back into two systems. Now one scan rates every carrier, buys the label and updates both.', 'The packer reads the address off one screen and types it into another, weighs the box and types that in too, compares carrier tabs by eye, then goes back to two systems to paste the tracking number. Per box.', '### The problem

Orders arrived in two places: a Shopify storefront and an on-premise invoice system. Shipping happened in a third place — whichever carrier portal was open in a browser tab.

So the packer''s job was transcription. Read the address off one screen, type it into the carrier''s site. Weigh the box, read the display, type the weight in. Open two more tabs to compare rates by eye. Buy the label. Click through a print dialog. Then go back to the storefront to mark the order fulfilled and paste the tracking number, and then into the invoice system to type the tracking number and the shipping cost onto the invoice. Per box — so a three-box order was three rounds of it.

The worst part was not the minutes. It was the ambiguity: if a label purchase timed out, nobody knew whether they had been charged. And nobody could say afterwards which employee had bought which label, or what a given order had actually cost to ship.

### What we built

One screen. Scan the order barcode and the shipment is assembled from whichever system it came from. The USB scale fills in the weight by itself, waiting for the reading to hold steady before it trusts it. Rates come back from every connected carrier account, sorted by price. One click buys. The label prints on the Zebra. The storefront is fulfilled and the customer notified, and the invoice record gets its tracking number and shipping cost. In auto mode, that is zero clicks between the scan and the printed label.

The engineering that matters here is the ordering, because this is a step that spends money. Label purchase is never retried blindly. Every provider is asked whether a label already exists before a buy is re-issued, and each buy carries an external reference the provider can deduplicate against. The guarantee in the code is one label per shipment, maximum: it can never double-charge.

Then the write-backs are made independent of the purchase. If the storefront or the invoice system is unreachable, the shipment simply sits at *label created* with the failure recorded, and a retry finishes the job later. A network problem downstream can cost you a minute. It can never cost you a purchased label.

Five carrier platforms sit behind one interface, with as many named accounts of each as the client wants. Weight and dimension units convert automatically — pounds and inches at the desk, whatever the carrier requires on the wire.

### The result

The shipping desk went from four screens to one. Address transcription is gone, weight transcription is gone, and the two systems that used to be updated by hand are updated by the same click that buys the label.

Voiding a label is now a genuine undo: the label is cancelled at the carrier, the fulfilment is removed from the storefront, and the invoice fields are cleared — but only if they still hold the value the system wrote, so a number a human typed afterwards is never wiped.

Because every label is attributed to the person who bought it, the client can finally answer questions they previously could not: what did we spend on shipping this week, with which carrier, and through whose station. End-of-day carrier manifests, which used to be assembled by hand, are generated and claimed atomically so two packing stations cannot manifest the same label twice.

### Why it holds up

- **Money-safe by construction.** Purchases are checked before they are re-issued, write-backs retry independently, and a void retries only the steps that actually failed.
- **113 automated tests** across the provider adapters, tag rules and write-back logic, running with no database attached.
- **Twenty tracked SQL migrations** applied in order at startup, including one that backfilled every historical shipment with a readable carrier name so old records stay legible after an integration is removed.
- **An installer that tunes itself.** It reads the host''s memory and derives container limits and database settings from it, backs up the database before every update, and offers a final backup before removal.', '<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>Orders arrived in two places: a Shopify storefront and an on-premise invoice system. Shipping happened in a third place — whichever carrier portal was open in a browser tab.</p>
<p>So the packer&rsquo;s job was transcription. Read the address off one screen, type it into the carrier&rsquo;s site. Weigh the box, read the display, type the weight in. Open two more tabs to compare rates by eye. Buy the label. Click through a print dialog. Then go back to the storefront to mark the order fulfilled and paste the tracking number, and then into the invoice system to type the tracking number and the shipping cost onto the invoice. Per box — so a three-box order was three rounds of it.</p>
<p>The worst part was not the minutes. It was the ambiguity: if a label purchase timed out, nobody knew whether they had been charged. And nobody could say afterwards which employee had bought which label, or what a given order had actually cost to ship.</p>
<h3>What we built</h3>
<p>One screen. Scan the order barcode and the shipment is assembled from whichever system it came from. The USB scale fills in the weight by itself, waiting for the reading to hold steady before it trusts it. Rates come back from every connected carrier account, sorted by price. One click buys. The label prints on the Zebra. The storefront is fulfilled and the customer notified, and the invoice record gets its tracking number and shipping cost. In auto mode, that is zero clicks between the scan and the printed label.</p>
<p>The engineering that matters here is the ordering, because this is a step that spends money. Label purchase is never retried blindly. Every provider is asked whether a label already exists before a buy is re-issued, and each buy carries an external reference the provider can deduplicate against. The guarantee in the code is one label per shipment, maximum: it can never double-charge.</p>
<p>Then the write-backs are made independent of the purchase. If the storefront or the invoice system is unreachable, the shipment simply sits at <em>label created</em> with the failure recorded, and a retry finishes the job later. A network problem downstream can cost you a minute. It can never cost you a purchased label.</p>
<p>Five carrier platforms sit behind one interface, with as many named accounts of each as the client wants. Weight and dimension units convert automatically — pounds and inches at the desk, whatever the carrier requires on the wire.</p>
<h3>The result</h3>
<p>The shipping desk went from four screens to one. Address transcription is gone, weight transcription is gone, and the two systems that used to be updated by hand are updated by the same click that buys the label.</p>
<p>Voiding a label is now a genuine undo: the label is cancelled at the carrier, the fulfilment is removed from the storefront, and the invoice fields are cleared — but only if they still hold the value the system wrote, so a number a human typed afterwards is never wiped.</p>
<p>Because every label is attributed to the person who bought it, the client can finally answer questions they previously could not: what did we spend on shipping this week, with which carrier, and through whose station. End-of-day carrier manifests, which used to be assembled by hand, are generated and claimed atomically so two packing stations cannot manifest the same label twice.</p>
<h3>Why it holds up</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Money-safe by construction.</strong> Purchases are checked before they are re-issued, write-backs retry independently, and a void retries only the steps that actually failed.</li>
<li><strong>113 automated tests</strong> across the provider adapters, tag rules and write-back logic, running with no database attached.</li>
<li><strong>Twenty tracked SQL migrations</strong> applied in order at startup, including one that backfilled every historical shipment with a readable carrier name so old records stay legible after an integration is removed.</li>
<li><strong>An installer that tunes itself.</strong> It reads the host&rsquo;s memory and derives container limits and database settings from it, backs up the database before every update, and offers a final backup before removal.</li>
</ul>',
