-- 3. One barcode truth across every system
INSERT INTO case_studies
  (title, slug, client, industry, engagement, summary, problem_lede, body_md, body_html,
   cover_image, cover_thumb, gallery, metrics, tech_tags, stack, meta_title, meta_description,
   sort_order, is_featured, is_published, published_at)
VALUES ('One barcode truth across every system', 'barcode-price-integrity', 'Multi-company distributor running several back offices and storefronts', 'Distribution & Wholesale', 'Eleven months and counting · ongoing',
  'The same product had different barcodes and different prices in every system. One console now corrects a barcode everywhere at once and sets a price for every channel.', 'Changing one barcode meant opening five systems and editing it by hand — and every quote, purchase order and invoice ever raised still carried the old one, forever.', '### The problem

Several trading companies, each with its own back-office database. Several storefronts on top of them. One physical catalogue underneath all of it — and no agreement anywhere about what a given product''s barcode was.

Changing a barcode meant opening every system and editing it by hand, then discovering that history did not follow: quotes, purchase orders, invoices, credit memos, returns and bin locations all kept the old barcode forever. So old invoices referenced items the catalogue could no longer find, and nobody could reconcile them.

Prices were the same story in a different key. A new cost meant re-keying a price ladder into five places, each with its own markup, done on a calculator, and getting it identical every time. It was never identical.

And the question the owner most wanted answered — what did we actually sell last month, across all of it — could not be answered at all, because the two halves of the business counted differently and neither knew about the other.

### What we built

One console. Type a barcode, see every place it exists, change it once.

Underneath, a search fans out across every connected back office and queries eight tables per database — the item catalogue plus every transaction detail table that has ever referenced that barcode — and every storefront variant alongside them. The stores are queried concurrently, so the wait is the slowest system rather than the sum of all of them. The rewrite is batched to the two thousand keys a SQL Server statement can carry, wrapped in a transaction, and rolled back on any error.

The behaviour that took the longest to get right is the one that matters most in practice: if the new barcode already exists on one storefront, **that store alone is skipped** with a recorded reason, and every other system still gets the update. Blocking the whole operation because of one collision was the original design, and it was wrong — a partial success recorded honestly is worth far more than an all-or-nothing failure.

There is also a reconciliation engine for the mess that already exists. It scans the transaction tables in thousand-row chunks looking for **orphans** — barcodes that history references but the catalogue has lost — and repairs them by matching on product ID or description, one committed write per row so a single bad record cannot roll back the rest. Progress streams to the screen while it runs.

Prices move the same way: one entry, four tiers, every back office and every storefront, with mirror stores following automatically and a full before-and-after audit row for each.

### The result

The catalogue tells one story. A barcode correction takes one action instead of five, and it reaches history as well as the current record, which is what makes old paperwork reconcilable again. Price changes are entered once and land everywhere at the store''s own markup.

Because the system keeps a local mirror of storefront orders and customers alongside the back-office invoices, the reporting screens can finally merge both halves of the business on one consistent rule for what counts as a completed sale — in the shop''s own timezone, which is the detail that quietly ruins most attempts at this.

Nearly five hundred commits in, it is the deepest system in our portfolio and still shipping. That is not a boast about volume; it is what a system looks like when the client keeps finding new things worth asking it.

### Why it holds up

- **Every change is auditable.** Barcode and price updates are grouped under a batch identifier and record the old value, the new value, the table, the affected keys and — for skips and failures — the reason. Price history keeps before and after for all four tiers.
- **The health check tells the truth.** It reports live database pool usage and returns a failure when the pool is saturated, so a saturated system reads as unhealthy rather than as a hang. A watchdog container restarts the service when it does.
- **Rate limits are learned, not assumed.** The storefront client mirrors the platform''s own credit accounting from every response, holds a reserve, and backs off with jitter so parallel jobs do not all wake at once and re-throttle.
- **Twenty-nine idempotent migrations** applied in order on every update, with the database volume preserved — and one command to install, update or remove the whole stack.', '<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>Several trading companies, each with its own back-office database. Several storefronts on top of them. One physical catalogue underneath all of it — and no agreement anywhere about what a given product&rsquo;s barcode was.</p>
<p>Changing a barcode meant opening every system and editing it by hand, then discovering that history did not follow: quotes, purchase orders, invoices, credit memos, returns and bin locations all kept the old barcode forever. So old invoices referenced items the catalogue could no longer find, and nobody could reconcile them.</p>
<p>Prices were the same story in a different key. A new cost meant re-keying a price ladder into five places, each with its own markup, done on a calculator, and getting it identical every time. It was never identical.</p>
<p>And the question the owner most wanted answered — what did we actually sell last month, across all of it — could not be answered at all, because the two halves of the business counted differently and neither knew about the other.</p>
<h3>What we built</h3>
<p>One console. Type a barcode, see every place it exists, change it once.</p>
<p>Underneath, a search fans out across every connected back office and queries eight tables per database — the item catalogue plus every transaction detail table that has ever referenced that barcode — and every storefront variant alongside them. The stores are queried concurrently, so the wait is the slowest system rather than the sum of all of them. The rewrite is batched to the two thousand keys a SQL Server statement can carry, wrapped in a transaction, and rolled back on any error.</p>
<p>The behaviour that took the longest to get right is the one that matters most in practice: if the new barcode already exists on one storefront, <strong>that store alone is skipped</strong> with a recorded reason, and every other system still gets the update. Blocking the whole operation because of one collision was the original design, and it was wrong — a partial success recorded honestly is worth far more than an all-or-nothing failure.</p>
<p>There is also a reconciliation engine for the mess that already exists. It scans the transaction tables in thousand-row chunks looking for <strong>orphans</strong> — barcodes that history references but the catalogue has lost — and repairs them by matching on product ID or description, one committed write per row so a single bad record cannot roll back the rest. Progress streams to the screen while it runs.</p>
<p>Prices move the same way: one entry, four tiers, every back office and every storefront, with mirror stores following automatically and a full before-and-after audit row for each.</p>
<h3>The result</h3>
<p>The catalogue tells one story. A barcode correction takes one action instead of five, and it reaches history as well as the current record, which is what makes old paperwork reconcilable again. Price changes are entered once and land everywhere at the store&rsquo;s own markup.</p>
<p>Because the system keeps a local mirror of storefront orders and customers alongside the back-office invoices, the reporting screens can finally merge both halves of the business on one consistent rule for what counts as a completed sale — in the shop&rsquo;s own timezone, which is the detail that quietly ruins most attempts at this.</p>
<p>Nearly five hundred commits in, it is the deepest system in our portfolio and still shipping. That is not a boast about volume; it is what a system looks like when the client keeps finding new things worth asking it.</p>
<h3>Why it holds up</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Every change is auditable.</strong> Barcode and price updates are grouped under a batch identifier and record the old value, the new value, the table, the affected keys and — for skips and failures — the reason. Price history keeps before and after for all four tiers.</li>
<li><strong>The health check tells the truth.</strong> It reports live database pool usage and returns a failure when the pool is saturated, so a saturated system reads as unhealthy rather than as a hang. A watchdog container restarts the service when it does.</li>
<li><strong>Rate limits are learned, not assumed.</strong> The storefront client mirrors the platform&rsquo;s own credit accounting from every response, holds a reserve, and backs off with jitter so parallel jobs do not all wake at once and re-throttle.</li>
<li><strong>Twenty-nine idempotent migrations</strong> applied in order on every update, with the database volume preserved — and one command to install, update or remove the whole stack.</li>
</ul>',
  '/static/media/work/barcode-price-integrity/cover.webp', '/static/media/work/barcode-price-integrity/cover-thumb.webp', '[{"src": "/static/media/work/barcode-price-integrity/01-price-reconciliation.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/barcode-price-integrity/01-price-reconciliation-thumb.webp", "caption": "One product, fifteen storefronts, every price and cost side by side before a single update"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/barcode-price-integrity/02-orphan-upc-audit.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/barcode-price-integrity/02-orphan-upc-audit-thumb.webp", "caption": "Orphaned barcodes found in quotations, invoices and purchase orders that no longer exist in the catalog"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/barcode-price-integrity/03-live-operations.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/barcode-price-integrity/03-live-operations-thumb.webp", "caption": "Live picking and packing across four back-office databases, line items and scan timeline included"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/barcode-price-integrity/04-fulfillment-status.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/barcode-price-integrity/04-fulfillment-status-thumb.webp", "caption": "1,560 open orders across eight storefronts, split by where each one is stuck"}]'::jsonb, '[{"value": "15", "label": "storefronts kept in step"}, {"value": "8", "label": "tables corrected per barcode"}, {"value": "4", "label": "price tiers, one entry"}, {"value": "16", "label": "screens in one console"}]'::jsonb,
