VALUES ('Add a product once, everywhere', 'product-onboarding-automation', 'Multi-company wholesaler selling through several storefronts', 'Retail & E-commerce', '10 weeks · ongoing support',
  'Adding one product meant typing 58 fields into every system by hand and getting them identical. Now it is one form, and every destination gets its own prices and categories.', 'To add one new product, somebody typed the same fifty-eight fields into every back office and every storefront, with different category codes in each, and a calculator open for the price ladder.', '### The problem

"To add one new product I have to type it into every one of our systems, by hand, and get it identical every time."

The item record has fifty-eight fields. Each back-office company has its own category tree, its own manufacturer list and its own units, so four of those fields are different in every system even though the product is the same. Each one also has its own markup, so the price ladder — cost, price, cash and carry, two delivery tiers, list price — was worked out on a calculator per destination.

Then the same product again in each storefront: title, description, vendor, type, tags, search listing, custom fields, collections, variants, weight, stock per location, and publishing to each sales channel. Plus watermarking the photographs first.

It took most of an afternoon per product, and the duplicate barcodes it created were discovered weeks later.

### What we built

One form. Enter the product, tick the destinations, submit.

Prices are declarative rather than manual. Each destination has formulas, and every formula calculates from the base cost you typed — deliberately without chaining. If cost is 100 and the store''s rules are cost × 1.05 and price × 1.20, the price is 120, not 126. That single decision — no formula reads another formula''s output — is what makes the numbers explainable to the person who has to defend them, and it is enforced on the server, not in the browser.

Validation happens before anything is written. Every barcode and SKU is checked against every destination — an exact match, and separately a match on the first fourteen characters of the SKU, because this client''s SKUs share a stem across pack sizes and a stem collision is a real conflict even when the full code differs. A destination that already has the product is skipped with a specific reason while every other destination still receives the insert. Partial success, reported honestly, beats an all-or-nothing failure.

Two more things removed real hours. **Sibling mode**: give it an existing barcode and three new identity fields, and it clones the entire fifty-eight-field record per destination, zeroing the stock and history fields — which is how "add the sixteen-ounce version of this" stopped being an afternoon. And **bulk import**: a supplier spreadsheet is uploaded, its columns recognised from forty-four accepted header spellings, its categories matched per destination, and every row pre-checked for duplicates — including duplicates within the file itself — before a single write. Imports run in the background with live progress, and every row goes through exactly the same code path as a manual entry, so the same validation and the same audit trail apply.

### The result

Product onboarding went from most of an afternoon to a single form submission, with the price ladder computed per destination instead of by calculator.

Duplicate barcodes are caught at entry rather than discovered weeks later, and they no longer block the destinations that are fine. Supplier price lists are imported instead of re-keyed row by row.

Every submission — manual or imported — is logged with the full form data, the destinations targeted, the ones that succeeded and the ones that failed with their reasons. The history screen reports a run that reached four of six destinations as **partial**, not as a pass or a failure, because that is what actually happened and the person looking needs to know which two to chase.

### Why it holds up

- **Migrations self-heal.** They re-run at every startup rather than trusting a tracking table, serialised across workers with a database lock, and are written to never take the application down if one fails.
- **Credentials are encrypted at rest.** Back-office passwords and storefront tokens are stored encrypted, and never returned by any interface.
- **It degrades instead of breaking.** If the storefront rejects a consolidated query, the client falls back to individual ones and reports which parts failed; if an API version is retired, it automatically retries against the previous two.
- **One command to install, update or remove,** with the database preserved across updates and a status screen showing the running version, the configured destinations and the active pricing formulas.', '<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>&ldquo;To add one new product I have to type it into every one of our systems, by hand, and get it identical every time.&rdquo;</p>
<p>The item record has fifty-eight fields. Each back-office company has its own category tree, its own manufacturer list and its own units, so four of those fields are different in every system even though the product is the same. Each one also has its own markup, so the price ladder — cost, price, cash and carry, two delivery tiers, list price — was worked out on a calculator per destination.</p>
<p>Then the same product again in each storefront: title, description, vendor, type, tags, search listing, custom fields, collections, variants, weight, stock per location, and publishing to each sales channel. Plus watermarking the photographs first.</p>
<p>It took most of an afternoon per product, and the duplicate barcodes it created were discovered weeks later.</p>
<h3>What we built</h3>
<p>One form. Enter the product, tick the destinations, submit.</p>
<p>Prices are declarative rather than manual. Each destination has formulas, and every formula calculates from the base cost you typed — deliberately without chaining. If cost is 100 and the store&rsquo;s rules are cost × 1.05 and price × 1.20, the price is 120, not 126. That single decision — no formula reads another formula&rsquo;s output — is what makes the numbers explainable to the person who has to defend them, and it is enforced on the server, not in the browser.</p>
<p>Validation happens before anything is written. Every barcode and SKU is checked against every destination — an exact match, and separately a match on the first fourteen characters of the SKU, because this client&rsquo;s SKUs share a stem across pack sizes and a stem collision is a real conflict even when the full code differs. A destination that already has the product is skipped with a specific reason while every other destination still receives the insert. Partial success, reported honestly, beats an all-or-nothing failure.</p>
<p>Two more things removed real hours. <strong>Sibling mode</strong>: give it an existing barcode and three new identity fields, and it clones the entire fifty-eight-field record per destination, zeroing the stock and history fields — which is how &ldquo;add the sixteen-ounce version of this&rdquo; stopped being an afternoon. And <strong>bulk import</strong>: a supplier spreadsheet is uploaded, its columns recognised from forty-four accepted header spellings, its categories matched per destination, and every row pre-checked for duplicates — including duplicates within the file itself — before a single write. Imports run in the background with live progress, and every row goes through exactly the same code path as a manual entry, so the same validation and the same audit trail apply.</p>
<h3>The result</h3>
<p>Product onboarding went from most of an afternoon to a single form submission, with the price ladder computed per destination instead of by calculator.</p>
<p>Duplicate barcodes are caught at entry rather than discovered weeks later, and they no longer block the destinations that are fine. Supplier price lists are imported instead of re-keyed row by row.</p>
<p>Every submission — manual or imported — is logged with the full form data, the destinations targeted, the ones that succeeded and the ones that failed with their reasons. The history screen reports a run that reached four of six destinations as <strong>partial</strong>, not as a pass or a failure, because that is what actually happened and the person looking needs to know which two to chase.</p>
<h3>Why it holds up</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Migrations self-heal.</strong> They re-run at every startup rather than trusting a tracking table, serialised across workers with a database lock, and are written to never take the application down if one fails.</li>
<li><strong>Credentials are encrypted at rest.</strong> Back-office passwords and storefront tokens are stored encrypted, and never returned by any interface.</li>
<li><strong>It degrades instead of breaking.</strong> If the storefront rejects a consolidated query, the client falls back to individual ones and reports which parts failed; if an API version is retired, it automatically retries against the previous two.</li>
<li><strong>One command to install, update or remove,</strong> with the database preserved across updates and a status screen showing the running version, the configured destinations and the active pricing formulas.</li>
</ul>',
