-- 5. Four scan stations, one shared source of truth
INSERT INTO case_studies
  (title, slug, client, industry, engagement, summary, problem_lede, body_md, body_html,
   cover_image, cover_thumb, gallery, metrics, tech_tags, stack, meta_title, meta_description,
   sort_order, is_featured, is_published, published_at)
VALUES ('Four scan stations, one shared source of truth', 'warehouse-floor-suite', 'Distribution warehouse running an on-premise ERP', 'Warehousing & Logistics', 'Four apps over ten months · ongoing support',
  'Receiving, bin locations, packing and cycle counts all ran on paper and memory. Four small scan apps now write straight into the existing ERP, with a name on every entry.', 'A short delivery was found six weeks later, at reconciliation. Nobody could say who packed the order that went out wrong. And the count that would have caught both takes the building offline for a day.', '### The problem

Four separate jobs on one warehouse floor, all of them running on paper, memory and the two people who knew how it really worked.

**Receiving** was a printed purchase order and a pen. Short deliveries were discovered at month end, long after the supplier would credit them. **Bin locations** lived in one person''s head, so a new picker walked the aisles looking for stock the system said existed somewhere. **Packing** had no record of who did it, so a wrongly packed order was an unattributed mystery and nobody learned anything from it. **Cycle counting** happened once a year, took the building offline, and produced numbers that were already stale by the time they were keyed in.

Every one of these was the same problem wearing different clothes: work happened away from a desk, and the record of it was written down later, by somebody else, from memory.

### What we built

Four small applications, one per job, all writing into the ERP the client already runs.

At **receiving**, the operator opens the purchase order on a tablet and scans the delivery. Case barcodes are resolved to their unit barcode and case quantity automatically, so a pallet of cases does not have to be counted in pieces. Over-receiving is flagged at the door. When every line is complete, the session closes itself after a five-second countdown.

**Bin locations** gives a searchable map of what is in which slot, with quick plus and minus adjustments made in the aisle and an Excel export for anyone who still wants the list on paper.

The **pack station** is deliberately the simplest thing in the building: tap your name, scan the order. The first scan is the packer signing for it; the second is the checker confirming it; a third is refused. Two timestamps and a name, written into the ERP''s own columns.

**Cycle counts** run on a phone. Scan the shelf tag and the screen shows the bin it should be in, the units already committed to open orders, and what is inbound on purchase orders — so the counter is not counting blind. Enter cases and loose pieces and the app shows the arithmetic back in plain language before committing. A change larger than a configurable threshold stops and asks.

The technical decision worth naming: where these apps have logins, they authenticate against the ERP''s own user tables rather than keeping their own. There is no second list of staff to maintain, and disabling somebody in the ERP locks them out of the floor immediately.

### The result

Discrepancies are caught at the door instead of at reconciliation, while the supplier will still credit them. Every quantity change carries a name and a timestamp, written into the client''s own audit table so it appears in their existing reporting with no integration work. Counts happen continuously on a phone instead of once a year with the doors shut.

The four apps never talk to each other. They interlock through the ERP: the count screen reads the pack station''s timestamps to warn that an item is being picked right now, reads the bin app''s data to tell the counter where to look, and reads the receiving app''s updates to flag stock that arrived this morning. One source of truth, four windows onto it, no middleware to go wrong.

### Why it holds up

- **Writes are transactional and reversible.** Receiving updates the order line, the order header and the item quantity as a gated chain; anything that fails goes to a durable retry queue rather than disappearing. Every posted receipt can be undone, and the undo is hardened against partial failure.
- **Multiple passes do not clobber each other.** The receiving app records the ERP''s starting quantity the first time it touches a line, so a second delivery against the same order adds to the first instead of overwriting it.
- **Clocks are handled deliberately.** Timestamps are pinned to the warehouse''s own timezone, and one app takes its time from SQL Server itself so its history lines up exactly with the ERP''s.
- **Each app installs, updates and removes with one command,** backing up its data before every update, with health checks and automatic restart on all containers.', '<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>Four separate jobs on one warehouse floor, all of them running on paper, memory and the two people who knew how it really worked.</p>
<p><strong>Receiving</strong> was a printed purchase order and a pen. Short deliveries were discovered at month end, long after the supplier would credit them. <strong>Bin locations</strong> lived in one person&rsquo;s head, so a new picker walked the aisles looking for stock the system said existed somewhere. <strong>Packing</strong> had no record of who did it, so a wrongly packed order was an unattributed mystery and nobody learned anything from it. <strong>Cycle counting</strong> happened once a year, took the building offline, and produced numbers that were already stale by the time they were keyed in.</p>
<p>Every one of these was the same problem wearing different clothes: work happened away from a desk, and the record of it was written down later, by somebody else, from memory.</p>
<h3>What we built</h3>
<p>Four small applications, one per job, all writing into the ERP the client already runs.</p>
<p>At <strong>receiving</strong>, the operator opens the purchase order on a tablet and scans the delivery. Case barcodes are resolved to their unit barcode and case quantity automatically, so a pallet of cases does not have to be counted in pieces. Over-receiving is flagged at the door. When every line is complete, the session closes itself after a five-second countdown.</p>
<p><strong>Bin locations</strong> gives a searchable map of what is in which slot, with quick plus and minus adjustments made in the aisle and an Excel export for anyone who still wants the list on paper.</p>
<p>The <strong>pack station</strong> is deliberately the simplest thing in the building: tap your name, scan the order. The first scan is the packer signing for it; the second is the checker confirming it; a third is refused. Two timestamps and a name, written into the ERP&rsquo;s own columns.</p>
<p><strong>Cycle counts</strong> run on a phone. Scan the shelf tag and the screen shows the bin it should be in, the units already committed to open orders, and what is inbound on purchase orders — so the counter is not counting blind. Enter cases and loose pieces and the app shows the arithmetic back in plain language before committing. A change larger than a configurable threshold stops and asks.</p>
<p>The technical decision worth naming: where these apps have logins, they authenticate against the ERP&rsquo;s own user tables rather than keeping their own. There is no second list of staff to maintain, and disabling somebody in the ERP locks them out of the floor immediately.</p>
<h3>The result</h3>
<p>Discrepancies are caught at the door instead of at reconciliation, while the supplier will still credit them. Every quantity change carries a name and a timestamp, written into the client&rsquo;s own audit table so it appears in their existing reporting with no integration work. Counts happen continuously on a phone instead of once a year with the doors shut.</p>
<p>The four apps never talk to each other. They interlock through the ERP: the count screen reads the pack station&rsquo;s timestamps to warn that an item is being picked right now, reads the bin app&rsquo;s data to tell the counter where to look, and reads the receiving app&rsquo;s updates to flag stock that arrived this morning. One source of truth, four windows onto it, no middleware to go wrong.</p>
<h3>Why it holds up</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Writes are transactional and reversible.</strong> Receiving updates the order line, the order header and the item quantity as a gated chain; anything that fails goes to a durable retry queue rather than disappearing. Every posted receipt can be undone, and the undo is hardened against partial failure.</li>
<li><strong>Multiple passes do not clobber each other.</strong> The receiving app records the ERP&rsquo;s starting quantity the first time it touches a line, so a second delivery against the same order adds to the first instead of overwriting it.</li>
<li><strong>Clocks are handled deliberately.</strong> Timestamps are pinned to the warehouse&rsquo;s own timezone, and one app takes its time from SQL Server itself so its history lines up exactly with the ERP&rsquo;s.</li>
<li><strong>Each app installs, updates and removes with one command,</strong> backing up its data before every update, with health checks and automatic restart on all containers.</li>
</ul>',
  '/static/media/work/warehouse-floor-suite/cover.webp', '/static/media/work/warehouse-floor-suite/cover-thumb.webp', '[{"src": "/static/media/work/warehouse-floor-suite/01-bin-adjust.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/warehouse-floor-suite/01-bin-adjust-thumb.webp", "caption": "Adjust a bin in two keystrokes — with a reason recorded against the change."}, {"src": "/static/media/work/warehouse-floor-suite/02-receiving-scan.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/warehouse-floor-suite/02-receiving-scan-thumb.webp", "caption": "Receiving mid-count: 638 of 722 units verified, with the short lines highlighted as they are scanned."}, {"src": "/static/media/work/warehouse-floor-suite/03-receiving-summary.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/warehouse-floor-suite/03-receiving-summary-thumb.webp", "caption": "The close-out a buyer can act on: 84 units short across two lines, itemised against the PO."}, {"src": "/static/media/work/warehouse-floor-suite/04-pack-station.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/warehouse-floor-suite/04-pack-station-thumb.webp", "caption": "Pack station scan confirmed and stamped to the packer — first and second touches tracked per order."}, {"src": "/static/media/work/warehouse-floor-suite/05-mobile-cycle-count.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/warehouse-floor-suite/05-mobile-cycle-count-thumb.webp", "caption": "Cycle counting on a phone: cases times pack size, with open orders and receipts shown before the count commits."}]'::jsonb, '[{"value": "4", "label": "apps, one shared truth"}, {"value": "0", "label": "new staff accounts to manage"}, {"value": "3", "label": "ERP tables per scan"}, {"value": "Same day", "label": "short deliveries caught"}]'::jsonb,
