VALUES ('Reorder points that follow real demand', 'demand-driven-purchasing', 'Regional food and consumer-goods distributor', 'Distribution & Wholesale', '8 weeks · six months of live tuning',
  'Buying ran on a reorder field somebody typed in years ago. Now every threshold comes from what actually sold, and a supplier order is two clicks.', 'The reorder level in the system was a number a person typed once, years ago, for a product that sells nothing like it used to. Everyone knew it was wrong, so everyone ignored it and bought from memory instead.', '### The problem

Buying was a morning of guesswork. Pull an on-hand report. Eyeball what looks low. Try to remember how fast each item actually moves, and whether there is already a truck coming with some of it, and whether a customer quote is holding some more. Divide by the case pack in your head. Retype the whole thing into a spreadsheet, format it, email it to the vendor — then key the same purchase order into the ERP a second time by hand.

The system did have a reorder level field. It was a number somebody typed in years ago, when the product sold differently, and everybody knew it was wrong. So the real reorder logic lived in the buyer''s head, which meant it left the building when he did.

### What we built

A purchasing screen where the threshold is computed rather than remembered.

For every product, the system reads real invoice history over a chosen window — thirty, sixty, ninety or a hundred and eighty days — and derives daily and monthly sell-through, excluding voided invoices. The reorder threshold is one month of demand, rounded up.

The trigger is not naive on-hand, and that is the part buyers care about:

```
effective_qty = on_hand + open_purchase_orders - quotations_in_progress
```

Stock already on the way counts. Stock already promised to a customer does not. An item only appears on the buy list when that effective figure falls below its threshold, which is why the list stopped including things that were already handled.

The suggested quantity covers a configurable period — typically four weeks — and is rounded **up to whole cases**, because ordering 37 units of a product that ships in cases of 12 is not an order anybody can place.

Then the buyer''s judgement gets a home. Seven per-product levers: pin a manual threshold, fall back to the ERP''s own number, exclude an item from ordering entirely while still tracking it, override the quantity, set a longer coverage period for a slow-turning line, fix a unit cost, or leave a note. That knowledge used to live in one head. It now survives that person''s vacation.

Finishing the order is two clicks: an Excel or PDF the vendor can read, and a real purchase order written straight into the ERP — product descriptions re-read live at export time, so the document never ships a stale name.

### The result

The buy list is derived from what sold, not from a field nobody trusts. Stock already inbound and stock already committed are both accounted for before an item is suggested, so the list is short and the items on it are real.

The order leaves the building as a formatted supplier document and lands in the ERP as a proper purchase order in the same action — the double keying is gone.

Six of the first seven months after launch were spent tuning it against live buying, which is the pattern we look for: a short build, then a long conversation with the people who use it every morning.

### Why it holds up

- **The money math is unit-tested.** The velocity and case-rounding logic has its own test suite covering the awkward cases — zero sales history, a zero day-span, an item sitting exactly on its threshold, and inclusive date boundaries.
- **Database failures retry rather than crash.** A decorator wraps every ERP query with three retries at one, two and four seconds, distinguishing a timeout from a connection failure from a genuinely bad query, which fails fast instead of retrying pointlessly.
- **Migrations run themselves.** Schema changes are idempotent and applied on startup, so an update never needs a manual database step.
- **One command to install, update or remove,** preserving the database across updates and asking before it deletes anything.', '<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>Buying was a morning of guesswork. Pull an on-hand report. Eyeball what looks low. Try to remember how fast each item actually moves, and whether there is already a truck coming with some of it, and whether a customer quote is holding some more. Divide by the case pack in your head. Retype the whole thing into a spreadsheet, format it, email it to the vendor — then key the same purchase order into the ERP a second time by hand.</p>
<p>The system did have a reorder level field. It was a number somebody typed in years ago, when the product sold differently, and everybody knew it was wrong. So the real reorder logic lived in the buyer&rsquo;s head, which meant it left the building when he did.</p>
<h3>What we built</h3>
<p>A purchasing screen where the threshold is computed rather than remembered.</p>
<p>For every product, the system reads real invoice history over a chosen window — thirty, sixty, ninety or a hundred and eighty days — and derives daily and monthly sell-through, excluding voided invoices. The reorder threshold is one month of demand, rounded up.</p>
<p>The trigger is not naive on-hand, and that is the part buyers care about:</p>
<pre><code>effective_qty = on_hand + open_purchase_orders - quotations_in_progress
</code></pre>
<p>Stock already on the way counts. Stock already promised to a customer does not. An item only appears on the buy list when that effective figure falls below its threshold, which is why the list stopped including things that were already handled.</p>
<p>The suggested quantity covers a configurable period — typically four weeks — and is rounded <strong>up to whole cases</strong>, because ordering 37 units of a product that ships in cases of 12 is not an order anybody can place.</p>
<p>Then the buyer&rsquo;s judgement gets a home. Seven per-product levers: pin a manual threshold, fall back to the ERP&rsquo;s own number, exclude an item from ordering entirely while still tracking it, override the quantity, set a longer coverage period for a slow-turning line, fix a unit cost, or leave a note. That knowledge used to live in one head. It now survives that person&rsquo;s vacation.</p>
<p>Finishing the order is two clicks: an Excel or PDF the vendor can read, and a real purchase order written straight into the ERP — product descriptions re-read live at export time, so the document never ships a stale name.</p>
<h3>The result</h3>
<p>The buy list is derived from what sold, not from a field nobody trusts. Stock already inbound and stock already committed are both accounted for before an item is suggested, so the list is short and the items on it are real.</p>
<p>The order leaves the building as a formatted supplier document and lands in the ERP as a proper purchase order in the same action — the double keying is gone.</p>
<p>Six of the first seven months after launch were spent tuning it against live buying, which is the pattern we look for: a short build, then a long conversation with the people who use it every morning.</p>
<h3>Why it holds up</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>The money math is unit-tested.</strong> The velocity and case-rounding logic has its own test suite covering the awkward cases — zero sales history, a zero day-span, an item sitting exactly on its threshold, and inclusive date boundaries.</li>
<li><strong>Database failures retry rather than crash.</strong> A decorator wraps every ERP query with three retries at one, two and four seconds, distinguishing a timeout from a connection failure from a genuinely bad query, which fails fast instead of retrying pointlessly.</li>
<li><strong>Migrations run themselves.</strong> Schema changes are idempotent and applied on startup, so an update never needs a manual database step.</li>
<li><strong>One command to install, update or remove,</strong> preserving the database across updates and asking before it deletes anything.</li>
</ul>',
