VALUES ('916 catalogue pages, scored and rewritten', 'ai-seo-catalog', 'Single-brand online retailer with a large catalogue', 'Retail & E-commerce', '6 months · ongoing content operations',
  'A 900-page catalogue nobody could edit by hand. Every page is now scored against explicit rules, rewritten by AI agents, and approved by a human before publishing.', 'Somebody exported the catalogue to a spreadsheet, hand-wrote meta descriptions one product at a time, and gave up around item eighty. Alt text never got written at all.', '### The problem

Nine hundred and sixteen pages. Seven hundred and eighteen products, a hundred and ninety-two collections, and a handful of pages and articles.

The old job was a spreadsheet or an agency retainer. Export the catalogue, scan for blank meta descriptions, sort by title length to find the ones that will get truncated, hand-write a description, paste it back into the admin one product at a time, and stop somewhere around item eighty. Duplicate title tags were invisible unless somebody ran a pivot table — and the worst duplicates, the ones caused by a *blank* SEO title silently falling back to the product name, were invisible even then. Alt text never got written. The keyword data sat in one browser tab and the search-console data in another, and nobody joined them.

Meanwhile the storefront itself was carrying a duplicated analytics script on every page load and a reviews widget loading on pages that had no reviews.

### What we built

A system that scores the catalogue, ranks what is wrong, fixes it with AI agents, and pushes the result to the storefront — with a human approving every published change.

Scoring is explicit and weighted rather than a black box. Products are scored on four content checks — meta title at 50 to 60 characters, meta description at 120 to 160, body at 300-plus words, and image alt coverage — plus thirteen keyword-alignment checks covering keyword placement, density, heading structure and URL. Every threshold is an editable setting, not a hardcoded constant, and checks that do not apply are removed from the denominator rather than scored as zero, so a product with no images is not punished for missing alt text.

Twenty named issue types are ranked by severity, each carrying two separate scopes: which fields an AI fix is permitted to write, and which failing checks the model is allowed to see. Those scopes exist because five different issues all rewrite the description, and a single shared scope made "fix all" quietly rewrite everything.

The agents run a tool-calling loop against the store''s own verified data, with a must-preserve keyword list so an existing ranking survives the rewrite, and an output guard that **strips any quote the model could not have taken verbatim from its inputs**. The specification table may contain only provided facts. Nothing invented gets published.

Two supporting pieces earn their place. Duplicate detection compares the *rendered* title — falling back to the product name when the SEO title is blank — which is the only way to catch the duplicates that matter. And content-gap discovery joins keyword data against thirty days of Search Console metrics, then runs a cannibalisation check that refuses to recommend an article competing with a page the store already ranks with.

### The result

All 916 pages are scored and re-scored, with the caches for an item invalidated the moment it changes so a re-check never benchmarks against a stale page.

On the storefront itself, an independent audit produced measurable, verifiable movement: the product page''s layout shift fell from 0.221 to 0.006 — a 97% reduction — taking its performance score from 77 to 86 in a single verified fix. The Lighthouse SEO category scores **100 out of 100** on every page type. Weighted speed across product, collection and home pages moved from the high seventies into the mid eighties, against a platform theme-store minimum of 60.

Search Console tells the slower story: 923 of 1,187 submitted URLs indexed, average position improved by nearly two places, and the AI-written blog section growing 41% in clicks — the only section growing at all.

### Why it holds up

- **Rate limits are handled properly.** Platform throttling arrives as a success response with an error inside it; the client detects that, computes the wait from the platform''s own credit telemetry, and honours retry headers with a thirty-second cap.
- **Exactly one process runs the schedulers,** elected through the database, so publishing, data pulls and snapshots cannot double-fire no matter how many workers are running.
- **Failures cannot hang.** A job that dies is marked failed rather than left running forever, stale jobs are swept, and a failed analysis short-circuits before the paid generation call whose output would be discarded.
- **69 tests on the pure logic** — link classification, media specs, mention linking and candidate selection — deliberately kept free of database and network.', '<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>Nine hundred and sixteen pages. Seven hundred and eighteen products, a hundred and ninety-two collections, and a handful of pages and articles.</p>
<p>The old job was a spreadsheet or an agency retainer. Export the catalogue, scan for blank meta descriptions, sort by title length to find the ones that will get truncated, hand-write a description, paste it back into the admin one product at a time, and stop somewhere around item eighty. Duplicate title tags were invisible unless somebody ran a pivot table — and the worst duplicates, the ones caused by a <em>blank</em> SEO title silently falling back to the product name, were invisible even then. Alt text never got written. The keyword data sat in one browser tab and the search-console data in another, and nobody joined them.</p>
<p>Meanwhile the storefront itself was carrying a duplicated analytics script on every page load and a reviews widget loading on pages that had no reviews.</p>
<h3>What we built</h3>
<p>A system that scores the catalogue, ranks what is wrong, fixes it with AI agents, and pushes the result to the storefront — with a human approving every published change.</p>
<p>Scoring is explicit and weighted rather than a black box. Products are scored on four content checks — meta title at 50 to 60 characters, meta description at 120 to 160, body at 300-plus words, and image alt coverage — plus thirteen keyword-alignment checks covering keyword placement, density, heading structure and URL. Every threshold is an editable setting, not a hardcoded constant, and checks that do not apply are removed from the denominator rather than scored as zero, so a product with no images is not punished for missing alt text.</p>
<p>Twenty named issue types are ranked by severity, each carrying two separate scopes: which fields an AI fix is permitted to write, and which failing checks the model is allowed to see. Those scopes exist because five different issues all rewrite the description, and a single shared scope made &ldquo;fix all&rdquo; quietly rewrite everything.</p>
<p>The agents run a tool-calling loop against the store&rsquo;s own verified data, with a must-preserve keyword list so an existing ranking survives the rewrite, and an output guard that <strong>strips any quote the model could not have taken verbatim from its inputs</strong>. The specification table may contain only provided facts. Nothing invented gets published.</p>
<p>Two supporting pieces earn their place. Duplicate detection compares the <em>rendered</em> title — falling back to the product name when the SEO title is blank — which is the only way to catch the duplicates that matter. And content-gap discovery joins keyword data against thirty days of Search Console metrics, then runs a cannibalisation check that refuses to recommend an article competing with a page the store already ranks with.</p>
<h3>The result</h3>
<p>All 916 pages are scored and re-scored, with the caches for an item invalidated the moment it changes so a re-check never benchmarks against a stale page.</p>
<p>On the storefront itself, an independent audit produced measurable, verifiable movement: the product page&rsquo;s layout shift fell from 0.221 to 0.006 — a 97% reduction — taking its performance score from 77 to 86 in a single verified fix. The Lighthouse SEO category scores <strong>100 out of 100</strong> on every page type. Weighted speed across product, collection and home pages moved from the high seventies into the mid eighties, against a platform theme-store minimum of 60.</p>
<p>Search Console tells the slower story: 923 of 1,187 submitted URLs indexed, average position improved by nearly two places, and the AI-written blog section growing 41% in clicks — the only section growing at all.</p>
<h3>Why it holds up</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Rate limits are handled properly.</strong> Platform throttling arrives as a success response with an error inside it; the client detects that, computes the wait from the platform&rsquo;s own credit telemetry, and honours retry headers with a thirty-second cap.</li>
<li><strong>Exactly one process runs the schedulers,</strong> elected through the database, so publishing, data pulls and snapshots cannot double-fire no matter how many workers are running.</li>
<li><strong>Failures cannot hang.</strong> A job that dies is marked failed rather than left running forever, stale jobs are swept, and a failed analysis short-circuits before the paid generation call whose output would be discarded.</li>
<li><strong>69 tests on the pure logic</strong> — link classification, media specs, mention linking and candidate selection — deliberately kept free of database and network.</li>
</ul>',
  '/static/media/work/ai-seo-catalog/cover.webp', '/static/media/work/ai-seo-catalog/cover-thumb.webp', '[{"src": "/static/media/work/ai-seo-catalog/01-opportunity-queue.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/ai-seo-catalog/01-opportunity-queue-thumb.webp", "caption": "Every page ranked by opportunity, with the exact keyword and the clicks it is worth"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/ai-seo-catalog/02-ai-search-readiness.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/ai-seo-catalog/02-ai-search-readiness-thumb.webp", "caption": "Readiness, feed health and share of voice across 360 AI answers, scored and queued"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/ai-seo-catalog/03-keyword-strategy.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/ai-seo-catalog/03-keyword-strategy-thumb.webp", "caption": "10,692 keywords clustered into 48 families, showing the volume each content type is failing to cover"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/ai-seo-catalog/04-search-console.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/ai-seo-catalog/04-search-console-thumb.webp", "caption": "Search Console pulled every six hours: 203,783 rows of clicks, impressions and position"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/ai-seo-catalog/05-image-manager.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/ai-seo-catalog/05-image-manager-thumb.webp", "caption": "749 products audited for missing images, placeholders and alt-text gaps"}]'::jsonb, '[{"value": "916", "label": "catalogue pages scored"}, {"value": "100/100", "label": "Lighthouse SEO score"}, {"value": "97%", "label": "less layout shift"}, {"value": "20", "label": "issue types tracked"}]'::jsonb,
  ARRAY['Python', 'AI agents', 'Shopify API', 'PostgreSQL', 'Search Console']::text[], ARRAY['Python 3.11 · Flask · gunicorn', 'PostgreSQL 15 with 73 tables and 75 idempotent migrations', 'OpenRouter with a live, switchable model catalogue', 'Shopify Admin GraphQL API 2026-01', 'Google Search Console and Semrush data joined', 'Leader-elected background schedulers', 'nginx · Docker Compose · healthchecks']::text[], 'AI catalogue SEO with a human in the loop', '916 pages scored against weighted rules, 20 issue types ranked, AI agents writing titles and alt text — nothing published without approval.',
