VALUES ('Intake without the clipboard or the callback', 'legal-intake-portal', 'Personal-injury law firm', 'Legal Services', '6 weeks · ongoing support',
  'Callers answered thirty questions to a receptionist and got no number. Now the questionnaire runs itself, explains a range on screen, and opens a tracked case.', 'An injured caller answers the same thirty questions out loud to a receptionist, is told an attorney will call back, and hangs up with no idea what their claim might be worth or what happens next.', '### The problem

Intake was a phone call and a clipboard.

Somebody injured calls the firm. A receptionist takes them through thirty-odd questions verbally. They are told an attorney will call back, and they hang up knowing nothing: not what their claim might be worth, not what happens next, not whether the firm is even taking it. A paralegal then re-keys the notes, looks up the state''s comparative-fault rule and limitation period in a binder, and forms an opinion.

Every "what''s happening with my case?" call after that is answered by hand.

And the questionnaire itself lived in a Word document. Changing a question meant a developer ticket, so it never changed.

### What we built

A guided questionnaire the client completes with no login at all, which ends in a plain-language summary and an indicative range — on screen, in seconds, with the reasoning shown.

That range is not a lookup table. It runs a six-stage pipeline. An extraction stage reads the answers into a strict structured schema. A jurisdiction stage applies that state''s own rules — comparative-fault regime, limitation period, non-economic damages cap — from a fifty-state table, and can stop the process outright where a legal gate blocks it. Comparable outcomes, an independent judgement stage and a deliberately adversarial "lowest defensible number" stage run concurrently.

Then, critically, **every dollar figure is computed in code, not by a model.** Undocumented losses are weighted down before anything is multiplied — undocumented medical at half, unreported cash wages excluded entirely. Contributory-negligence states collapse the range when the claimant shares fault. The width of the range widens with how much information is missing, clamped between five and sixty percent, and the result is rounded to a defensible increment. Confidence is derived from that width and stated openly, alongside the drivers, the reducers, and what the client could provide to improve the estimate. The whole intermediate trace is stored for the firm to inspect.

The lead becomes a client account through an emailed link, and the case moves through six named stages the client can see: New, Under review, Documents needed, Negotiating, Settled, Closed. Every stage change and every note posted by staff sends an email automatically.

The questionnaire itself is now the firm''s to change. A partner drags questions into order, groups them onto pages, and splits or merges pages in the browser. The layout is stored as ordering and grouping on the questions themselves and written through a single endpoint that validates the payload matches the existing questions exactly — no developer, and no way to lose a question by dragging.

### The result

A potential client gets a transparent, explained answer in the same session instead of waiting for a callback, which is the difference between a lead and a lost one. Intake staff stop transcribing and start reviewing.

The pipeline stage is visible to the client, so the "any update?" calls fall away on their own. Every notification that goes out is logged with its outcome, so nobody has to wonder whether the client was told.

And the questionnaire is a living document again. Because it can be changed in an afternoon, it gets changed — which is worth more than any single question on it.

### Why it holds up

- **397 automated tests**, weighted towards exactly the parts that must not drift: the damages arithmetic, the jurisdiction gates, the pipeline orchestration, and the drag-and-drop layout logic.
- **The pipeline heals itself.** A run whose heartbeat goes stale is failed automatically, so a deployment mid-estimate cannot leave a case stuck waiting forever.
- **Security is not an afterthought.** Argon2 hashing, tokens stored only as hashes, seven-day claim links and one-hour password resets, rate limiting on intake and login, automatic TLS with HSTS and a strict content-security policy — and the application refuses to start in production on a default secret.
- **One command to install, update or remove,** with migrations applied and seed data refreshed idempotently.', '<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>Intake was a phone call and a clipboard.</p>
<p>Somebody injured calls the firm. A receptionist takes them through thirty-odd questions verbally. They are told an attorney will call back, and they hang up knowing nothing: not what their claim might be worth, not what happens next, not whether the firm is even taking it. A paralegal then re-keys the notes, looks up the state&rsquo;s comparative-fault rule and limitation period in a binder, and forms an opinion.</p>
<p>Every &ldquo;what&rsquo;s happening with my case?&rdquo; call after that is answered by hand.</p>
<p>And the questionnaire itself lived in a Word document. Changing a question meant a developer ticket, so it never changed.</p>
<h3>What we built</h3>
<p>A guided questionnaire the client completes with no login at all, which ends in a plain-language summary and an indicative range — on screen, in seconds, with the reasoning shown.</p>
<p>That range is not a lookup table. It runs a six-stage pipeline. An extraction stage reads the answers into a strict structured schema. A jurisdiction stage applies that state&rsquo;s own rules — comparative-fault regime, limitation period, non-economic damages cap — from a fifty-state table, and can stop the process outright where a legal gate blocks it. Comparable outcomes, an independent judgement stage and a deliberately adversarial &ldquo;lowest defensible number&rdquo; stage run concurrently.</p>
<p>Then, critically, <strong>every dollar figure is computed in code, not by a model.</strong> Undocumented losses are weighted down before anything is multiplied — undocumented medical at half, unreported cash wages excluded entirely. Contributory-negligence states collapse the range when the claimant shares fault. The width of the range widens with how much information is missing, clamped between five and sixty percent, and the result is rounded to a defensible increment. Confidence is derived from that width and stated openly, alongside the drivers, the reducers, and what the client could provide to improve the estimate. The whole intermediate trace is stored for the firm to inspect.</p>
<p>The lead becomes a client account through an emailed link, and the case moves through six named stages the client can see: New, Under review, Documents needed, Negotiating, Settled, Closed. Every stage change and every note posted by staff sends an email automatically.</p>
<p>The questionnaire itself is now the firm&rsquo;s to change. A partner drags questions into order, groups them onto pages, and splits or merges pages in the browser. The layout is stored as ordering and grouping on the questions themselves and written through a single endpoint that validates the payload matches the existing questions exactly — no developer, and no way to lose a question by dragging.</p>
<h3>The result</h3>
<p>A potential client gets a transparent, explained answer in the same session instead of waiting for a callback, which is the difference between a lead and a lost one. Intake staff stop transcribing and start reviewing.</p>
<p>The pipeline stage is visible to the client, so the &ldquo;any update?&rdquo; calls fall away on their own. Every notification that goes out is logged with its outcome, so nobody has to wonder whether the client was told.</p>
<p>And the questionnaire is a living document again. Because it can be changed in an afternoon, it gets changed — which is worth more than any single question on it.</p>
<h3>Why it holds up</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>397 automated tests</strong>, weighted towards exactly the parts that must not drift: the damages arithmetic, the jurisdiction gates, the pipeline orchestration, and the drag-and-drop layout logic.</li>
<li><strong>The pipeline heals itself.</strong> A run whose heartbeat goes stale is failed automatically, so a deployment mid-estimate cannot leave a case stuck waiting forever.</li>
<li><strong>Security is not an afterthought.</strong> Argon2 hashing, tokens stored only as hashes, seven-day claim links and one-hour password resets, rate limiting on intake and login, automatic TLS with HSTS and a strict content-security policy — and the application refuses to start in production on a default secret.</li>
<li><strong>One command to install, update or remove,</strong> with migrations applied and seed data refreshed idempotently.</li>
</ul>',
