-- 9. Statements in, categorised spending out
INSERT INTO case_studies
  (title, slug, client, industry, engagement, summary, problem_lede, body_md, body_html,
   cover_image, cover_thumb, gallery, metrics, tech_tags, stack, meta_title, meta_description,
   sort_order, is_featured, is_published, published_at)
VALUES ('Statements in, categorised spending out', 'statement-intelligence', 'Finance and accounting practice', 'Professional Services', '6 weeks · ongoing support',
  'Four banks, four export formats, one of them a scanned PDF. Now they are dropped in, recognised, OCR''d, de-duplicated and categorised from what you corrected last time.', 'Every bank exports a different shape, one of them only exports a scan, and somebody spends a day a month turning all of it into rows — then categorises the same coffee shop for the four-hundredth time.', '### The problem

The Sunday-afternoon spreadsheet.

Download twelve months of statements from four institutions. Discover each one exports a different column layout, that one calls the money-out column "paid out" and another calls it "debit", and that the credit-card statement is a PDF with no export at all — and on the older accounts, a scan of a PDF.

Retype or paste rows into a sheet. Flip the signs by hand on the card exports, because they list charges as positive. Tag the same coffee shop as Coffee for the four-hundredth time. Import the same month twice by accident and spend an hour finding out why the totals moved. And never notice the small subscription cancelled in March that is still billing.

### What we built

Drop the files in. That is the whole interface.

The importer recognises twelve named institutions — eight American and four Canadian — by scoring each file against declarative profiles rather than hardcoded parsers. A profile knows its header signatures, its sign convention, its currency columns and, for PDFs, its brand markers, line pattern and section headings. When nothing scores highly enough, a generic detector takes over with about sixty header synonyms across nine column roles, and elects a date format across the whole column rather than guessing row by row. Scanned PDFs are detected by character density and image coverage — so a scan buried inside an otherwise-readable PDF is caught too — and run through OCR before parsing, one job at a time so it cannot swamp the server.

Categorisation is a cascade, and the order is the point:

1. **Your rules** — contains, starts-with, equals or regular expression, with amount ranges and per-account scope. Confirmed outright.
2. **Learned merchant memory**, exact match. Confirmed. Every time you correct something, it learns.
3. **Fuzzy merchant memory**, at a deliberately high similarity cutoff so it matches near-identical names only and never a subset — the rule exists so that a ride-hailing charge is never confused with the same company''s food delivery.
4. **Built-in keyword hints** — several hundred merchant patterns, longest match wins.

Only what survives all four is offered to an AI model, in batches, and **an AI suggestion is never auto-confirmed**. Suggested rows do not move money in the totals until a human confirms them, and confirming one teaches the memory.

Duplicates are handled by fingerprinting each row with an occurrence counter, so a re-uploaded file or an overlapping statement period is caught while two genuinely identical same-day charges both survive.

### The result

A day a month of retyping became dropping files into a browser. Statements that previously could not be imported at all — the scans — import like everything else.

The queue is grouped by merchant, so one keystroke categorises thirty rows, and the system remembers it permanently. Because the memory and the rules run before any model does, the categorisation cost falls every month rather than staying flat.

Nothing silently overwrites: every categorisation change is recorded in an events table, and a suggestion sits visibly as a suggestion until someone accepts it.

### Why it holds up

- **409 automated tests** across parsing, categorisation, deduplication, reporting and the API, plus a separate black-box suite run against the live stack, contrast and accessibility gates included.
- **Interrupted work recovers.** Parsing runs in background threads that a worker restart waits for, and any statement whose thread died is reset at startup rather than sitting at "processing" forever.
- **The installer sizes itself to the machine,** deriving container memory limits and database tuning from the host, backing up the database and stored statements before every update, and issuing TLS certificates with automatic renewal.
- **Per-user isolation is enforced route by route** and gated in the test suite, not assumed.', '<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>The Sunday-afternoon spreadsheet.</p>
<p>Download twelve months of statements from four institutions. Discover each one exports a different column layout, that one calls the money-out column &ldquo;paid out&rdquo; and another calls it &ldquo;debit&rdquo;, and that the credit-card statement is a PDF with no export at all — and on the older accounts, a scan of a PDF.</p>
<p>Retype or paste rows into a sheet. Flip the signs by hand on the card exports, because they list charges as positive. Tag the same coffee shop as Coffee for the four-hundredth time. Import the same month twice by accident and spend an hour finding out why the totals moved. And never notice the small subscription cancelled in March that is still billing.</p>
<h3>What we built</h3>
<p>Drop the files in. That is the whole interface.</p>
<p>The importer recognises twelve named institutions — eight American and four Canadian — by scoring each file against declarative profiles rather than hardcoded parsers. A profile knows its header signatures, its sign convention, its currency columns and, for PDFs, its brand markers, line pattern and section headings. When nothing scores highly enough, a generic detector takes over with about sixty header synonyms across nine column roles, and elects a date format across the whole column rather than guessing row by row. Scanned PDFs are detected by character density and image coverage — so a scan buried inside an otherwise-readable PDF is caught too — and run through OCR before parsing, one job at a time so it cannot swamp the server.</p>
<p>Categorisation is a cascade, and the order is the point:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Your rules</strong> — contains, starts-with, equals or regular expression, with amount ranges and per-account scope. Confirmed outright.</li>
<li><strong>Learned merchant memory</strong>, exact match. Confirmed. Every time you correct something, it learns.</li>
<li><strong>Fuzzy merchant memory</strong>, at a deliberately high similarity cutoff so it matches near-identical names only and never a subset — the rule exists so that a ride-hailing charge is never confused with the same company&rsquo;s food delivery.</li>
<li><strong>Built-in keyword hints</strong> — several hundred merchant patterns, longest match wins.</li>
</ol>
<p>Only what survives all four is offered to an AI model, in batches, and <strong>an AI suggestion is never auto-confirmed</strong>. Suggested rows do not move money in the totals until a human confirms them, and confirming one teaches the memory.</p>
<p>Duplicates are handled by fingerprinting each row with an occurrence counter, so a re-uploaded file or an overlapping statement period is caught while two genuinely identical same-day charges both survive.</p>
<h3>The result</h3>
<p>A day a month of retyping became dropping files into a browser. Statements that previously could not be imported at all — the scans — import like everything else.</p>
<p>The queue is grouped by merchant, so one keystroke categorises thirty rows, and the system remembers it permanently. Because the memory and the rules run before any model does, the categorisation cost falls every month rather than staying flat.</p>
<p>Nothing silently overwrites: every categorisation change is recorded in an events table, and a suggestion sits visibly as a suggestion until someone accepts it.</p>
<h3>Why it holds up</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>409 automated tests</strong> across parsing, categorisation, deduplication, reporting and the API, plus a separate black-box suite run against the live stack, contrast and accessibility gates included.</li>
<li><strong>Interrupted work recovers.</strong> Parsing runs in background threads that a worker restart waits for, and any statement whose thread died is reset at startup rather than sitting at &ldquo;processing&rdquo; forever.</li>
<li><strong>The installer sizes itself to the machine,</strong> deriving container memory limits and database tuning from the host, backing up the database and stored statements before every update, and issuing TLS certificates with automatic renewal.</li>
<li><strong>Per-user isolation is enforced route by route</strong> and gated in the test suite, not assumed.</li>
</ul>',
  '/static/media/work/statement-intelligence/cover.webp', '/static/media/work/statement-intelligence/cover-thumb.webp', '[{"src": "/static/media/work/statement-intelligence/01-category-reports.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/statement-intelligence/01-category-reports-thumb.webp", "caption": "Twelve months of spending broken down by category, merchant and month"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/statement-intelligence/02-recurring-charges.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/statement-intelligence/02-recurring-charges-thumb.webp", "caption": "Every subscription and recurring charge found automatically, with the next expected date"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/statement-intelligence/03-review-queue.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/statement-intelligence/03-review-queue-thumb.webp", "caption": "One decision categorises every charge from the same merchant — 293 charges cleared in minutes"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/statement-intelligence/04-transaction-ledger.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/statement-intelligence/04-transaction-ledger-thumb.webp", "caption": "571 transactions from six accounts in one searchable ledger"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/statement-intelligence/05-statement-log.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/statement-intelligence/05-statement-log-thumb.webp", "caption": "Every uploaded statement, the bank it was matched to, and exactly what was imported"}]'::jsonb, '[{"value": "12", "label": "banks recognised"}, {"value": "4", "label": "stages before AI is asked"}, {"value": "0", "label": "duplicate rows imported"}, {"value": "409", "label": "automated tests"}]'::jsonb,
  ARRAY['Python', 'OCR', 'PostgreSQL', 'Docker', 'AI assist']::text[], ARRAY['Python 3.12 · Flask · gunicorn', 'PostgreSQL 16 with 9 SQL migrations', 'pdfplumber, OCRmyPDF and Tesseract for scanned statements', 'openpyxl and xlrd for modern and legacy Excel', 'rapidfuzz for merchant matching', 'OpenRouter for optional suggestions, never auto-confirmed', 'nginx · Docker Compose with per-service resource limits']::text[], 'Bank statements imported, OCR''d and categorised', 'Twelve banks recognised automatically, scanned PDFs OCR''d, duplicates caught, and a four-stage categorisation cascade that learns from your corrections.',
