-- 10. Websites the owner can actually edit
INSERT INTO case_studies
  (title, slug, client, industry, engagement, summary, problem_lede, body_md, body_html,
   cover_image, cover_thumb, gallery, metrics, tech_tags, stack, meta_title, meta_description,
   sort_order, is_featured, is_published, published_at)
VALUES ('Websites the owner can actually edit', 'owner-editable-website', 'An accountancy practice and a film production agency', 'Professional Services', '3 to 5 weeks each · ongoing support',
  'Two firms paid a developer to change a paragraph. Both now run their own sites — pages, media, navigation — and one moves 40 GB client files too.', 'Changing one sentence on the services page meant emailing a freelancer, waiting three weeks, and paying for a paragraph. So the site stopped changing, and then it stopped being true.', '### The problem

Two firms, one complaint. Every change to the website went through somebody else.

The accountancy practice paid a freelancer — or waited three weeks — every time a service description changed, a new staff bio went up, or the menu needed reordering. Contact enquiries arrived as forwarded emails and got lost in an inbox. The admin login was a password everybody knew.

The production agency had the website problem plus a bigger one: moving footage. A client with forty gigabytes of camera originals either bought a cloud storage seat, split the delivery across five transfer links that expired within the week, or drove a hard drive across town. When the browser tab crashed at eighty percent, the whole thing started again. Nobody could remember which client had sent which take.

### What we built

Two sites, one principle: everything that changes regularly is editable by the people who own it.

The accountancy site ships with a full admin: page creation and editing in a rich-text editor, publish and unpublish as a separate action from saving, a media library with alt text, a drag-to-reorder navigation builder that supports nested menus, editable site settings, mail configuration with a test-send button, and a read/unread lead inbox. The admin account is protected by an authenticator app — the enrolment QR code is generated on the server so no third party ever sees the secret — with a lockout after five failed attempts and sessions stored server-side rather than in a cookie.

The agency site adds the file problem. Its uploader is a small, deliberate protocol: register the file, then send it in 32 MB chunks, then complete. **The server, not the browser, owns the committed offset.** A chunk arriving at the wrong offset is rejected with the correct one, and the client adopts it and continues with backing-off retries. Before the offset advances, the file is flushed and synced to disk and truncated back to the last good position, so a crash costs at most one chunk. Registration is idempotent against a file fingerprint, which means a closed laptop, a browser restart or a dropped connection all resume exactly where they stopped. On completion the server re-reads the file and verifies its hash; a mismatch resets the upload rather than accepting bad bytes.

Client links are generated with a label, an expiry, a size quota and a file cap, are stored only as a hash — the URL is displayed once and cannot be read back out of the system — and can be revoked in one click. Downloads are handed off to the web server rather than occupying an application worker, which makes them resumable for free.

### The result

Both firms edit their own sites. Pages, images, navigation and settings change on the day someone decides they should, which means the site stays true rather than slowly going stale between retainer invoices.

Enquiries land in an inbox with a read state instead of an email thread. The accountancy site''s whole admin was built and shipped in a day and has needed two maintenance commits in the six months since — which is the correct amount of attention for a website.

The agency stopped paying per-seat for file transfer and stopped losing uploads at eighty percent. A producer sends one labelled link with an expiry and a quota, and the footage arrives verified.

### Why it holds up

- **Content is sanitised on the server.** A strict allowlist of tags and per-tag attributes, scheme-checked links, and scripts dropped with their contents — so a pasted document cannot introduce anything executable.
- **Uploads are crash-safe by ordering,** not by hope: truncate, write, sync, then advance the recorded offset. There is also a free-disk floor that refuses new chunks rather than filling the volume.
- **Migrations apply under a database lock,** so multiple workers starting at once cannot double-apply them, and the application refuses to seed an admin account without a supplied password.
- **One command handles install, update, SSL renewal and removal** — including a DNS pre-check before requesting a certificate, backups before every update, and automatic renewal that reloads only the web container for a few seconds.', '<h3>The problem</h3>
<p>Two firms, one complaint. Every change to the website went through somebody else.</p>
<p>The accountancy practice paid a freelancer — or waited three weeks — every time a service description changed, a new staff bio went up, or the menu needed reordering. Contact enquiries arrived as forwarded emails and got lost in an inbox. The admin login was a password everybody knew.</p>
<p>The production agency had the website problem plus a bigger one: moving footage. A client with forty gigabytes of camera originals either bought a cloud storage seat, split the delivery across five transfer links that expired within the week, or drove a hard drive across town. When the browser tab crashed at eighty percent, the whole thing started again. Nobody could remember which client had sent which take.</p>
<h3>What we built</h3>
<p>Two sites, one principle: everything that changes regularly is editable by the people who own it.</p>
<p>The accountancy site ships with a full admin: page creation and editing in a rich-text editor, publish and unpublish as a separate action from saving, a media library with alt text, a drag-to-reorder navigation builder that supports nested menus, editable site settings, mail configuration with a test-send button, and a read/unread lead inbox. The admin account is protected by an authenticator app — the enrolment QR code is generated on the server so no third party ever sees the secret — with a lockout after five failed attempts and sessions stored server-side rather than in a cookie.</p>
<p>The agency site adds the file problem. Its uploader is a small, deliberate protocol: register the file, then send it in 32 MB chunks, then complete. <strong>The server, not the browser, owns the committed offset.</strong> A chunk arriving at the wrong offset is rejected with the correct one, and the client adopts it and continues with backing-off retries. Before the offset advances, the file is flushed and synced to disk and truncated back to the last good position, so a crash costs at most one chunk. Registration is idempotent against a file fingerprint, which means a closed laptop, a browser restart or a dropped connection all resume exactly where they stopped. On completion the server re-reads the file and verifies its hash; a mismatch resets the upload rather than accepting bad bytes.</p>
<p>Client links are generated with a label, an expiry, a size quota and a file cap, are stored only as a hash — the URL is displayed once and cannot be read back out of the system — and can be revoked in one click. Downloads are handed off to the web server rather than occupying an application worker, which makes them resumable for free.</p>
<h3>The result</h3>
<p>Both firms edit their own sites. Pages, images, navigation and settings change on the day someone decides they should, which means the site stays true rather than slowly going stale between retainer invoices.</p>
<p>Enquiries land in an inbox with a read state instead of an email thread. The accountancy site&rsquo;s whole admin was built and shipped in a day and has needed two maintenance commits in the six months since — which is the correct amount of attention for a website.</p>
<p>The agency stopped paying per-seat for file transfer and stopped losing uploads at eighty percent. A producer sends one labelled link with an expiry and a quota, and the footage arrives verified.</p>
<h3>Why it holds up</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Content is sanitised on the server.</strong> A strict allowlist of tags and per-tag attributes, scheme-checked links, and scripts dropped with their contents — so a pasted document cannot introduce anything executable.</li>
<li><strong>Uploads are crash-safe by ordering,</strong> not by hope: truncate, write, sync, then advance the recorded offset. There is also a free-disk floor that refuses new chunks rather than filling the volume.</li>
<li><strong>Migrations apply under a database lock,</strong> so multiple workers starting at once cannot double-apply them, and the application refuses to seed an admin account without a supplied password.</li>
<li><strong>One command handles install, update, SSL renewal and removal</strong> — including a DNS pre-check before requesting a certificate, backups before every update, and automatic renewal that reloads only the web container for a few seconds.</li>
</ul>',
  '/static/media/work/owner-editable-website/cover.webp', '/static/media/work/owner-editable-website/cover-thumb.webp', '[{"src": "/static/media/work/owner-editable-website/01-public-homepage.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/owner-editable-website/01-public-homepage-thumb.webp", "caption": "The whole homepage: services, differentiators, contact — all of it owner-editable"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/owner-editable-website/02-page-editor.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/owner-editable-website/02-page-editor-thumb.webp", "caption": "The owner rewrites a service page in a rich-text editor, with SEO fields and publish state beside it"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/owner-editable-website/03-media-library.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/owner-editable-website/03-media-library-thumb.webp", "caption": "Upload once, reuse anywhere — the image library behind every page"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/owner-editable-website/04-dark-agency-site.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/owner-editable-website/04-dark-agency-site-thumb.webp", "caption": "The same CMS underneath a dark, cinematic agency site"}, {"src": "/static/media/work/owner-editable-website/05-agency-work-grid.webp", "thumb": "/static/media/work/owner-editable-website/05-agency-work-grid-thumb.webp", "caption": "Filterable project grid, posters and captions managed from the same admin"}]'::jsonb, '[{"value": "0", "label": "developer tickets to edit pages"}, {"value": "9", "label": "admin screens, no training"}, {"value": "200 GB", "label": "per client upload link"}, {"value": "32 MB", "label": "resumable upload chunks"}]'::jsonb,
