UPDATE services SET title='Systems Integration', summary='Make the systems you already pay for exchange data by themselves — orders, stock, prices, tracking — with retries, logging and no retyping.',
  body_md='Most companies do not need new software. They need their existing software to talk. We build the service that sits between your systems and moves the specific records a person is currently carrying by hand.

**Integrations we have shipped**

- Two SQL Server databases and a Shopify storefront, reconciled into one true sellable-stock number and pushed on a schedule
- Orders pulled from Shopify and from a back-office invoice system into one shipping desk, with tracking written back to both
- Barcodes and prices held consistent across multiple SQL Server backends and multiple storefronts at once
- A new SKU entered once and delivered to the ERP and the storefront with images, variants and per-store pricing

**Why ours keep running**

Writes are idempotent: sending the same value twice leaves the same result, so a retry can never corrupt a count. Rate limits are budgeted rather than discovered, with backoff on throttling. Money-critical steps are ordered so that the irreversible action happens first and the write-backs retry independently — buying a shipping label never depends on a downstream system being up.

Everything that moves is logged, so "why did this change at 3pm" is a ten-second answer instead of an argument.', body_html='<p>Most companies do not need new software. They need their existing software to talk. We build the service that sits between your systems and moves the specific records a person is currently carrying by hand.</p>
<p><strong>Integrations we have shipped</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Two SQL Server databases and a Shopify storefront, reconciled into one true sellable-stock number and pushed on a schedule</li>
<li>Orders pulled from Shopify and from a back-office invoice system into one shipping desk, with tracking written back to both</li>
<li>Barcodes and prices held consistent across multiple SQL Server backends and multiple storefronts at once</li>
<li>A new SKU entered once and delivered to the ERP and the storefront with images, variants and per-store pricing</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Why ours keep running</strong></p>
<p>Writes are idempotent: sending the same value twice leaves the same result, so a retry can never corrupt a count. Rate limits are budgeted rather than discovered, with backoff on throttling. Money-critical steps are ordered so that the irreversible action happens first and the write-backs retry independently — buying a shipping label never depends on a downstream system being up.</p>
<p>Everything that moves is logged, so &ldquo;why did this change at 3pm&rdquo; is a ten-second answer instead of an argument.</p>', is_published=true, updated_at=now()
WHERE slug='systems-integration';
UPDATE services SET title='Business Automation', summary='Remove the recurring work entirely — reconciliation, reordering, reporting, document generation — and give the hours back to your team.',
  body_md='Every business has jobs that eat hours and add nothing: the Monday report, the reorder spreadsheet, the copy-paste between two screens, the chase for a status update. Those are automation targets, and they are usually the cheapest work we do.

**Automations already running**

- Reorder thresholds computed from actual sales velocity, with per-product overrides and exclusions, exported as supplier-ready Excel and PDF
- Bank and card statements ingested from CSV, Excel and scanned PDF, with categorisation learned from what you corrected last time
- Catalogue pages scored against SEO rules and rewritten by AI agents — with a human approving every change before it is published
- Stock listings published and unpublished automatically as availability crosses zero

**Where the judgement goes**

Automation is only trusted if it knows what it does not know. Ours propose rather than decide wherever a mistake would be expensive: an AI-written product description is a suggestion until someone approves it; an unusual reorder quantity is flagged rather than sent. Confidence is earned by making the machine''s reasoning visible.

We start with the single task that costs your team the most hours, automate it end to end, and expand only once it has been boring for a month.', body_html='<p>Every business has jobs that eat hours and add nothing: the Monday report, the reorder spreadsheet, the copy-paste between two screens, the chase for a status update. Those are automation targets, and they are usually the cheapest work we do.</p>
<p><strong>Automations already running</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Reorder thresholds computed from actual sales velocity, with per-product overrides and exclusions, exported as supplier-ready Excel and PDF</li>
<li>Bank and card statements ingested from CSV, Excel and scanned PDF, with categorisation learned from what you corrected last time</li>
<li>Catalogue pages scored against SEO rules and rewritten by AI agents — with a human approving every change before it is published</li>
<li>Stock listings published and unpublished automatically as availability crosses zero</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Where the judgement goes</strong></p>
<p>Automation is only trusted if it knows what it does not know. Ours propose rather than decide wherever a mistake would be expensive: an AI-written product description is a suggestion until someone approves it; an unusual reorder quantity is flagged rather than sent. Confidence is earned by making the machine&rsquo;s reasoning visible.</p>
<p>We start with the single task that costs your team the most hours, automate it end to end, and expand only once it has been boring for a month.</p>', is_published=true, updated_at=now()
WHERE slug='business-automation';

INSERT INTO settings (key, value) VALUES ('about_md', 'Edge Bourne Consulting exists for one reason: most small and mid-size companies run on systems that don''t talk to each other. Orders live in one place, inventory in another, accounting in a third — and people fill the gaps by retyping data.

We build the connective tissue. Custom web and mobile applications, integrations between the platforms you already use, and automation that removes the repetitive work entirely.

**What we have actually built**

The portfolio on this site is production software, not concepts. A sellable-stock engine that reconciles two SQL Server databases against a storefront so a multi-store retailer stops overselling. A shipping desk that pulls orders from two systems, compares carrier rates and writes tracking back to both. A barcode and price authority that keeps several back offices and several storefronts telling the same story — nearly five hundred commits deep and still shipping. Four scan stations on one warehouse floor. A purchasing tool that decides what to buy from what actually sold.

Together those systems represent well over a thousand commits of production work across distribution, retail, warehousing, legal, finance and professional services.

**How we work**

- **Small, senior, direct.** You talk to the people building your system — no account managers, no hand-offs, no junior developer learning on your budget.
- **Built around your workflow.** We start from how your company actually operates. Our warehouse tools sign people in against the ERP user table the client already maintains, rather than creating a second list of staff to keep in step — that is the general principle, applied.
- **Boringly reliable.** Docker deployments with healthchecks. Plain-SQL migrations that run at boot. One-line installers for install, update and remove. Audit trails on anything that touches money or stock. Test suites where the logic is worth testing — the statement importer alone carries over two hundred and fifty unit tests.
- **Yours at the end.** You own the code and the data. It runs on your infrastructure if you want it to. Nothing we build requires us to stay.

**What we do not do**

We don''t take on work we''d have to learn on your budget, we don''t rebuild systems that are working, and we don''t sell a platform. If the honest answer to your problem is "buy the off-the-shelf tool," we''ll tell you that in the first conversation — and then quote for the integration that makes it fit.

If your team spends hours a week moving data between systems, that''s usually the first thing we fix — and it usually pays for itself within months.')
  ON CONFLICT (key) DO UPDATE SET value=EXCLUDED.value, updated_at=now();
