INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, excerpt, body_md, body_html, meta_description, is_published, published_at)
VALUES ('What re-typing one order actually costs you', 'cost-of-retyping-data', 'Everyone says re-keying an order takes a minute. Timed honestly it takes four to nine, and the typing is the cheapest part of the bill.', 'Ask an owner what it costs to re-type an order into a second system and you will usually get a shrug. "A minute, maybe two." That answer is why the problem survives for years.

Here is the arithmetic, done honestly.

### Start with the real minute count

A re-typed order is rarely one action. It is: open the second system, find or create the customer, key the line items, check the units, fix the one line that does not match, save, and go back to mark the first system as done. Time it with a stopwatch rather than from memory. In every warehouse and back office we have measured, the honest number lands between four and nine minutes — not one or two.

Take the low end. Four minutes, sixty orders a day, five days a week. That is twenty hours a week. One full-time person, doing nothing but reading a screen and typing what it says into another screen.

At a loaded cost of $25 an hour that is roughly $26,000 a year. It is a real number, it appears in no budget line, and no one owns it.

### Then add the error rate

Humans transcribing numbers get them wrong somewhere between half a percent and one percent of the time. Call it 0.5%. Sixty orders a day is about fifteen thousand a year, so seventy-five orders leave your building with something wrong on them.

The cost of a wrong order is not the typing. It is the phone call, the return label, the credit note, the second shipment, the apology, and — occasionally — the customer who does not come back. Most operators, pressed, will put the all-in cost of one wrong shipment somewhere north of $80. Seventy-five of those is another $6,000, and that assumes you catch them.

The transcription error is also the worst kind of error, because it is invisible at the point it happens. Nothing is broken. The screen looks right. It surfaces two weeks later as a stock count that will not reconcile.

### Now add the part nobody prices

The re-typing job is almost always done by one person who is unusually good at it. They know that the units are cases in one system and eaches in the other. They know that customer 4412 is really the same as customer 4412-B. They know which three products always need a manual override.

None of that is written down. It lives in one head.

When that person is on vacation, throughput halves and the error rate triples. When that person leaves, you discover that a documented process never existed — you had a person, and you called it a process. That is the risk that actually keeps owners up at night, and it does not show up in any hourly calculation.

### And the part that is hardest to see

Re-typing sets the pace of everything downstream. If orders are keyed once a day at 4pm, then your warehouse cannot pick before 4pm, your stock levels are a day stale, and your website is quoting availability it does not have. The delay is not the typing — it is the batching that typing forces on you.

We have watched a company move from a once-a-day manual push to a five-minute automatic sync and discover that the real win was not the twenty hours. It was that the website stopped overselling, because reserved stock now moved in near real time instead of overnight.

### What to do about it

You do not need a platform migration. You need the two systems to exchange the specific records that a person is currently carrying between them.

Three questions decide whether it is worth doing:

1. **Is the data already in both systems'' reach?** If system A has an API or a database you can read, and system B has an API or a database you can write, the job is tractable. Most business software from the last twenty years qualifies, including a surprising amount of the old stuff.
2. **Is there one canonical key?** A SKU, a UPC, an order number, a customer ID. If the two systems agree on one identifier, the integration is straightforward. If they do not, the first piece of work is agreeing on one — and that work is valuable on its own.
3. **What happens when it fails?** The answer must not be "silently." A good integration writes down every record it moved, retries what it can, and puts what it cannot in a queue a human can see.

### The honest test

Pick your most re-typed record. Count how many times a day someone reads it in one system and types it into another. Multiply by four minutes. Multiply by 250 days.

If the answer is more than a couple of weeks of somebody''s year, you are not looking at an annoyance. You are looking at an unbudgeted salary, an invisible error rate, and a single point of failure wearing a friendly face.

That is usually the first thing worth fixing, and it usually pays for itself long before the year is out.
', '<p>Ask an owner what it costs to re-type an order into a second system and you will usually get a shrug. &ldquo;A minute, maybe two.&rdquo; That answer is why the problem survives for years.</p>
<p>Here is the arithmetic, done honestly.</p>
<h3>Start with the real minute count</h3>
<p>A re-typed order is rarely one action. It is: open the second system, find or create the customer, key the line items, check the units, fix the one line that does not match, save, and go back to mark the first system as done. Time it with a stopwatch rather than from memory. In every warehouse and back office we have measured, the honest number lands between four and nine minutes — not one or two.</p>
<p>Take the low end. Four minutes, sixty orders a day, five days a week. That is twenty hours a week. One full-time person, doing nothing but reading a screen and typing what it says into another screen.</p>
<p>At a loaded cost of $25 an hour that is roughly $26,000 a year. It is a real number, it appears in no budget line, and no one owns it.</p>
<h3>Then add the error rate</h3>
<p>Humans transcribing numbers get them wrong somewhere between half a percent and one percent of the time. Call it 0.5%. Sixty orders a day is about fifteen thousand a year, so seventy-five orders leave your building with something wrong on them.</p>
<p>The cost of a wrong order is not the typing. It is the phone call, the return label, the credit note, the second shipment, the apology, and — occasionally — the customer who does not come back. Most operators, pressed, will put the all-in cost of one wrong shipment somewhere north of $80. Seventy-five of those is another $6,000, and that assumes you catch them.</p>
<p>The transcription error is also the worst kind of error, because it is invisible at the point it happens. Nothing is broken. The screen looks right. It surfaces two weeks later as a stock count that will not reconcile.</p>
<h3>Now add the part nobody prices</h3>
<p>The re-typing job is almost always done by one person who is unusually good at it. They know that the units are cases in one system and eaches in the other. They know that customer 4412 is really the same as customer 4412-B. They know which three products always need a manual override.</p>
<p>None of that is written down. It lives in one head.</p>
<p>When that person is on vacation, throughput halves and the error rate triples. When that person leaves, you discover that a documented process never existed — you had a person, and you called it a process. That is the risk that actually keeps owners up at night, and it does not show up in any hourly calculation.</p>
<h3>And the part that is hardest to see</h3>
<p>Re-typing sets the pace of everything downstream. If orders are keyed once a day at 4pm, then your warehouse cannot pick before 4pm, your stock levels are a day stale, and your website is quoting availability it does not have. The delay is not the typing — it is the batching that typing forces on you.</p>
<p>We have watched a company move from a once-a-day manual push to a five-minute automatic sync and discover that the real win was not the twenty hours. It was that the website stopped overselling, because reserved stock now moved in near real time instead of overnight.</p>
<h3>What to do about it</h3>
<p>You do not need a platform migration. You need the two systems to exchange the specific records that a person is currently carrying between them.</p>
<p>Three questions decide whether it is worth doing:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Is the data already in both systems&rsquo; reach?</strong> If system A has an API or a database you can read, and system B has an API or a database you can write, the job is tractable. Most business software from the last twenty years qualifies, including a surprising amount of the old stuff.</li>
<li><strong>Is there one canonical key?</strong> A SKU, a UPC, an order number, a customer ID. If the two systems agree on one identifier, the integration is straightforward. If they do not, the first piece of work is agreeing on one — and that work is valuable on its own.</li>
<li><strong>What happens when it fails?</strong> The answer must not be &ldquo;silently.&rdquo; A good integration writes down every record it moved, retries what it can, and puts what it cannot in a queue a human can see.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The honest test</h3>
<p>Pick your most re-typed record. Count how many times a day someone reads it in one system and types it into another. Multiply by four minutes. Multiply by 250 days.</p>
<p>If the answer is more than a couple of weeks of somebody&rsquo;s year, you are not looking at an annoyance. You are looking at an unbudgeted salary, an invisible error rate, and a single point of failure wearing a friendly face.</p>
<p>That is usually the first thing worth fixing, and it usually pays for itself long before the year is out.</p>',
  'The honest arithmetic of re-typing data between systems: hours, error rates, key-person risk, and the batching delay nobody prices.', true, now())
