INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, excerpt, body_md, body_html, meta_description, is_published, published_at)
VALUES ('The four thresholds where a spreadsheet stops working', 'when-spreadsheets-break', 'Spreadsheets do not fail gradually. They fail at four identifiable thresholds, and each one has a tell you can check this afternoon.', 'Nobody chooses a spreadsheet as their operating system. It happens by accretion. Someone builds a tracker for one job, it works, and five years later that file is how the company decides what to buy, what to ship and who to invoice.

The useful question is not "are spreadsheets bad." They are not. The question is: at what specific point does yours stop working, and how will you know you have passed it?

There are four thresholds, and they are more identifiable than people expect.

### Threshold one: two people editing at once

This one is arithmetic. A single-writer file is fine. The moment a second person needs to change it during the same working day, you have a concurrency problem, and there are only bad answers to it.

Cloud sheets defer this threshold, they do not remove it. They give you simultaneous editing without giving you record locking, validation, or any notion of who changed a cell and why. The failure mode is quiet: two people fix the same row differently, last write wins, and neither knows.

**The tell:** somebody in your company says "don''t touch the file, I''m in it." If that sentence exists, you have crossed threshold one.

### Threshold two: about 5,000 rows

The number varies with your machine, but the behaviour does not. Somewhere in the low thousands of rows with live formulas, three things happen together: the file gets slow enough that people avoid opening it, filters and sorts start being applied and left applied, and nobody re-reads the whole sheet any more.

That last one is the real problem. Under a few hundred rows, a person can scan the sheet and spot a wrong value. Past a few thousand, nobody ever sees the whole thing. Errors stop being caught by eye, and there is no other mechanism catching them, because a spreadsheet has no constraints. A cell that should hold a quantity will accept the word "backorder" and say nothing.

**The tell:** you have rows nobody has looked at in a year, and you would not bet money on their contents.

### Threshold three: the first VLOOKUP across files

The moment one sheet reads from another, you have built a database with no referential integrity, no transactions, and no backup strategy. It also means you now have a data model — you just have not written it down.

This threshold is worth watching because it is where the maintenance cost turns non-linear. Every new file added to the web multiplies the ways the web can break. Renaming a column in one place silently breaks a formula in another. Nothing errors. A number just goes wrong.

**The tell:** somebody has to open files in a particular order for the numbers to be right.

### Threshold four: the file has a keeper

This is the one that actually costs money. It arrives when the spreadsheet contains logic that only one person understands — a pricing rule buried in a nested IF, a column that must be cleared every Monday, a tab that looks unused but is not.

At that point the spreadsheet is no longer a document. It is an undocumented application with one maintainer, no source control, no tests and no backups, running a part of your business. If the maintainer leaves, you cannot hire a replacement, because the job description is "know this file."

**The tell:** one person''s vacation changes what the company can do that week.

### What crossing a threshold should actually trigger

Not a platform migration. The most common mistake we see is jumping from "the spreadsheet is straining" to "we need an ERP," which is a two-year project to solve a two-week problem.

Cross threshold one or two, and the answer is usually a small purpose-built tool: a real database table, a form with validation, a list view, and login. That is days of work, not months, and it eliminates the concurrency and integrity problems outright while keeping the process exactly as your team runs it.

Cross threshold three, and the useful move is deciding which system owns each piece of data. Not building anything yet — just answering "where does the true price live?" and "where does the true stock number live?" Half the spreadsheet web usually evaporates once those questions have answers.

Cross threshold four, and the priority is not efficiency, it is risk. Get the logic out of the one head and into something written down and testable, even if the interface stays humble.

### Keep the spreadsheet where it belongs

Spreadsheets remain the best tool ever built for exploring a question, modelling a scenario, and doing a one-off analysis. Those uses have no thresholds. Nothing accumulates.

The trouble only starts when a spreadsheet stops answering a question and starts holding a record — when it becomes the place the company looks to find out what is true. Records need constraints, history and access control. Spreadsheets have none of the three, by design.

So run the four tells. If you can name the person who owns the file, you already have your answer.
