VALUES ('Buy or build: an hour-long decision you can defend', 'buy-or-build-framework', 'Four questions and a scoring table that settle the buy-versus-build argument with evidence instead of whoever speaks most confidently in the meeting.', '"Should we buy something off the shelf or build it?" is the most consequential software question a mid-size company asks, and it is usually decided by whoever argues most confidently in the meeting.

It deserves better than that. Here is a framework that takes about an hour and produces an answer you can defend.

### First, reject the two lazy answers

**"Always buy — building is expensive and risky."** True for anything that is not specific to you. Nobody should build their own accounting system, email, or payroll. It is also how companies end up with eleven subscriptions that do not talk to each other and a person whose full-time job is moving data between them.

**"Always build — off-the-shelf never fits."** Usually said by someone who enjoys building. Every system you build is a system you maintain forever.

The real answer is almost always: buy the commodity, build the connective tissue and the parts that are genuinely yours.

### The four questions

Run your candidate process through these. Answer honestly, in writing.

**1. Is this process a differentiator or a utility?**
Does the way you do this contribute to why customers choose you? Your pick-and-pack sequence, your pricing rules, your customer onboarding might. Your general ledger does not.

**2. How much would you have to change to fit the product?**
Get specific. Not "some configuration" — list the actual steps your team would do differently. If the list is more than three steps and includes a step your customers would notice, that is a real cost.

**3. Does it need to touch systems you already run?**
Count them. A tool that lives on its own island can be bought cheaply. A tool that must read from your ERP and write to your store already needs custom work; the question then is whether you are buying a product plus integration, or building one thing.

**4. What is the cost of the workaround you are running today?**
Hours per week, error rate, and how many people know how it works. If the answer is "twenty hours and one person," the status quo is not free and should not be scored as if it were.

### The scoring table

| Signal | Points toward BUY | Points toward BUILD |
|---|---|---|
| Process is a commodity (payroll, GL, email, CRM basics) | Strong buy | — |
| Process is how you compete | — | Strong build |
| A product fits with zero workflow change | Strong buy | — |
| You would change 3+ steps to fit the product | — | Build |
| Only one system involved | Buy | — |
| Must read/write 2+ existing systems | — | Build the integration at minimum |
| Requirements are stable and industry-standard | Buy | — |
| Requirements change with your business | — | Build |
| Under ~5 users, occasional use | Strong buy | — |
| Daily use by a team, all day | — | Build is defensible |
| No one currently does this manually | Buy | — |
| Someone spends 10+ hrs/week on the workaround | — | Build |
| You have no one to maintain software | Strong buy | — |
| A vendor would hold data you must control | — | Build |

Count the column weights. A clear majority is your answer. A tie usually means the honest answer is a third option.

### The third option, which is usually right

Buy the platform. Build the fit.

Keep the commercial system you already pay for — the ERP, the store, the accounting package — and build the thin layer that makes it work the way your business works. That layer is small, it is genuinely yours, and it is where nearly all the value is.

In practice that looks like: a scan station that writes into your existing ERP tables and authenticates against your existing ERP users, so there is no second user list to manage. A sync service that computes your true sellable stock and pushes it to your storefront. A one-screen tool that replaces the six-system copy-and-paste job of adding a new product. None of these replace a platform. All of them remove a person-shaped gap between platforms.

### Three costs people forget on both sides

**Buying has a build cost.** Data migration, integration, training, and the process changes you swore you would not make. Budget it as real money.

**Building has a running cost.** Hosting, updates, and someone who can change it in two years. Ask any builder — internal or external — what happens when you need a change and they are unavailable. If there is no good answer, the price is wrong.

**Both have an exit cost.** With a vendor, ask how you get your data out and what it costs. With custom work, ask who owns the code and whether another developer could pick it up. "You own the repository, it runs in Docker, migrations are plain SQL" is a real answer. "It''s on our platform" is not.

### The one-hour version

Write the process on a page. Time the current workaround. Answer the four questions. Fill the table. If it says buy, buy without guilt. If it says build, scope the smallest version that removes the pain — one workflow, not a platform — and ship that first.

The companies that get this right are rarely the ones with the strongest opinion. They are the ones who wrote it down.
', '<p>&ldquo;Should we buy something off the shelf or build it?&rdquo; is the most consequential software question a mid-size company asks, and it is usually decided by whoever argues most confidently in the meeting.</p>
