INSERT INTO posts (title, slug, excerpt, body_md, body_html, meta_description, is_published, published_at)
VALUES ('The true cost of overselling, and the formula that ends it', 'the-oversell-problem', 'Cancellations are the cheapest part of overselling. The expensive part is the safety buffer you hold forever because you do not trust the number.', 'Overselling looks like a small problem. A customer orders something you do not have, you apologise, you refund, you move on. Once a week, maybe. Annoying, not fatal.

It is not a small problem, and the reason is that the visible cost is the smallest part of it.

### What an oversell actually costs

Start with the direct costs of a single incident:

- Someone finds it — usually a picker, usually after the order has been paid for.
- Someone calls or emails the customer. Ten minutes, plus the emotional overhead.
- You refund, or you substitute, or you back-order. Each of those has handling cost.
- If you have already shipped part of the order, you now have a split shipment you are paying for.

Call it thirty to sixty minutes of attention and a real cost somewhere between $30 and $100 before you count anything soft.

Now the parts that do not appear on an invoice:

**Marketplace penalties.** If you sell anywhere other than your own site, cancellation rate is a scored metric. Enough cancellations and you lose buy-box position, search placement, or the account. This is the cost that turns an annoyance into an existential problem, and it accrues silently until a threshold is crossed.

**Trust, unevenly distributed.** The customer who gets the cancellation is disproportionately likely to be a new customer, because your regulars buy the things you always have. So the cost lands hardest on exactly the relationships you spent marketing money to create.

**The defensive buffer.** This is the big one, and almost nobody counts it. After enough oversells, someone decides to hold back stock — list 90% of what you have, keep a cushion. That cushion is now permanently unsellable inventory. On a $1M inventory position, a 10% buffer is $100,000 of working capital doing nothing, forever, to compensate for a number you do not trust.

That is the real price of overselling: not the cancellations, but the capital you tie up to avoid them.

### Why the number is wrong in the first place

Almost always, one of four things:

1. **Stock is counted in one system and sold in another,** and the sync is a person, or a nightly job, or both.
2. **Reserved stock is invisible.** Units allocated to an open order, a pick in progress, or a wholesale hold are still counted as available.
3. **Reservations are per-store, not global.** A unit reserved by store A is still shown as sellable by store B. If you run more than one sales channel from one warehouse, this alone will oversell you steadily.
4. **Incoming stock is treated as here.** A confirmed purchase order is real, but it is not on the shelf, and mixing the two makes availability optimistic.

### The formula that ends it

The fix is to stop publishing "what the warehouse system says" and start publishing a computed number. In the systems we build, sellable stock is a single expression, evaluated per product, on a schedule:

```
sellable = max(0, on_hand + confirmed_po - in_progress - committed)
```

Four terms, and each one has to be defended:

- **on_hand** — physically in the building, per the system of record. One system of record. If two systems disagree about on_hand, that is a separate problem you must fix first.
- **confirmed_po** — stock on a confirmed purchase order. Include this only if you are willing to sell against it, and only if your PO confirmations are honest. Many operators set this term to zero, and that is a perfectly good answer.
- **in_progress** — units on picks, transfers or work in flight. Physically present, not available.
- **committed** — units allocated to open orders. This is the term that must be summed across **every** sales channel, not per store. A reservation anywhere is a reservation everywhere.

The `max(0, ...)` matters more than it looks. Without it, one bad count produces a negative number, and a negative number pushed into a storefront produces behaviour that ranges from "listing hidden" to "listing deleted." Clamp at zero and let the reconciliation happen in the warehouse, not on your product page.

The second half of the fix is what happens when the number hits zero: the listing is unpublished automatically, and republished automatically when stock returns. Manual publish states drift within a week, every time.

### Getting it right operationally

A correct formula pushed carelessly still causes trouble. Three rules that matter in practice:

**Idempotent writes.** Setting a product to 12 units must be safe to do twice. If your sync sends deltas instead of absolute values, a retry will corrupt the count. Send the number, not the change.

**Budget your API calls.** Storefront platforms rate-limit. A sync that ignores this gets throttled precisely when you have the most to update — during a busy day. Budget the calls, back off on 429s, and prioritise items that changed.

**Log every write.** When a merchandiser asks why a product went out of stock at 3pm, "here is the record: on_hand 40, committed 40, sellable 0, pushed at 15:02" ends the conversation in ten seconds. Without it, you are relitigating trust in the system every week.

### What good looks like

You stop hearing about oversells. Then, a few weeks later, someone asks whether you still need the safety buffer — and the answer is no, because the published number is now the true number.

That is where the money actually is.
', '<p>Overselling looks like a small problem. A customer orders something you do not have, you apologise, you refund, you move on. Once a week, maybe. Annoying, not fatal.</p>
<p>It is not a small problem, and the reason is that the visible cost is the smallest part of it.</p>
<h3>What an oversell actually costs</h3>
<p>Start with the direct costs of a single incident:</p>
<ul>
<li>Someone finds it — usually a picker, usually after the order has been paid for.</li>
<li>Someone calls or emails the customer. Ten minutes, plus the emotional overhead.</li>
<li>You refund, or you substitute, or you back-order. Each of those has handling cost.</li>
<li>If you have already shipped part of the order, you now have a split shipment you are paying for.</li>
</ul>
<p>Call it thirty to sixty minutes of attention and a real cost somewhere between $30 and $100 before you count anything soft.</p>
<p>Now the parts that do not appear on an invoice:</p>
<p><strong>Marketplace penalties.</strong> If you sell anywhere other than your own site, cancellation rate is a scored metric. Enough cancellations and you lose buy-box position, search placement, or the account. This is the cost that turns an annoyance into an existential problem, and it accrues silently until a threshold is crossed.</p>
<p><strong>Trust, unevenly distributed.</strong> The customer who gets the cancellation is disproportionately likely to be a new customer, because your regulars buy the things you always have. So the cost lands hardest on exactly the relationships you spent marketing money to create.</p>
<p><strong>The defensive buffer.</strong> This is the big one, and almost nobody counts it. After enough oversells, someone decides to hold back stock — list 90% of what you have, keep a cushion. That cushion is now permanently unsellable inventory. On a $1M inventory position, a 10% buffer is $100,000 of working capital doing nothing, forever, to compensate for a number you do not trust.</p>
<p>That is the real price of overselling: not the cancellations, but the capital you tie up to avoid them.</p>
<h3>Why the number is wrong in the first place</h3>
<p>Almost always, one of four things:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Stock is counted in one system and sold in another,</strong> and the sync is a person, or a nightly job, or both.</li>
<li><strong>Reserved stock is invisible.</strong> Units allocated to an open order, a pick in progress, or a wholesale hold are still counted as available.</li>
<li><strong>Reservations are per-store, not global.</strong> A unit reserved by store A is still shown as sellable by store B. If you run more than one sales channel from one warehouse, this alone will oversell you steadily.</li>
<li><strong>Incoming stock is treated as here.</strong> A confirmed purchase order is real, but it is not on the shelf, and mixing the two makes availability optimistic.</li>
</ol>
<h3>The formula that ends it</h3>
<p>The fix is to stop publishing &ldquo;what the warehouse system says&rdquo; and start publishing a computed number. In the systems we build, sellable stock is a single expression, evaluated per product, on a schedule:</p>
<pre><code>sellable = max(0, on_hand + confirmed_po - in_progress - committed)
</code></pre>
<p>Four terms, and each one has to be defended:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>on_hand</strong> — physically in the building, per the system of record. One system of record. If two systems disagree about on_hand, that is a separate problem you must fix first.</li>
<li><strong>confirmed_po</strong> — stock on a confirmed purchase order. Include this only if you are willing to sell against it, and only if your PO confirmations are honest. Many operators set this term to zero, and that is a perfectly good answer.</li>
<li><strong>in_progress</strong> — units on picks, transfers or work in flight. Physically present, not available.</li>
<li><strong>committed</strong> — units allocated to open orders. This is the term that must be summed across <strong>every</strong> sales channel, not per store. A reservation anywhere is a reservation everywhere.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <code>max(0, ...)</code> matters more than it looks. Without it, one bad count produces a negative number, and a negative number pushed into a storefront produces behaviour that ranges from &ldquo;listing hidden&rdquo; to &ldquo;listing deleted.&rdquo; Clamp at zero and let the reconciliation happen in the warehouse, not on your product page.</p>
<p>The second half of the fix is what happens when the number hits zero: the listing is unpublished automatically, and republished automatically when stock returns. Manual publish states drift within a week, every time.</p>
<h3>Getting it right operationally</h3>
<p>A correct formula pushed carelessly still causes trouble. Three rules that matter in practice:</p>
<p><strong>Idempotent writes.</strong> Setting a product to 12 units must be safe to do twice. If your sync sends deltas instead of absolute values, a retry will corrupt the count. Send the number, not the change.</p>
<p><strong>Budget your API calls.</strong> Storefront platforms rate-limit. A sync that ignores this gets throttled precisely when you have the most to update — during a busy day. Budget the calls, back off on 429s, and prioritise items that changed.</p>
<p><strong>Log every write.</strong> When a merchandiser asks why a product went out of stock at 3pm, &ldquo;here is the record: on_hand 40, committed 40, sellable 0, pushed at 15:02&rdquo; ends the conversation in ten seconds. Without it, you are relitigating trust in the system every week.</p>
<h3>What good looks like</h3>
<p>You stop hearing about oversells. Then, a few weeks later, someone asks whether you still need the safety buffer — and the answer is no, because the published number is now the true number.</p>
<p>That is where the money actually is.</p>',
  'Why overselling costs far more than cancellations, the four reasons stock numbers go wrong, and the sellable-stock formula that fixes them.', true, now())
